window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2026-06-2", "source": "2026年06月真题·第2套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay that begins with the sentence \"In an era of rapid social development, it is vitally important to enhance young people's sense of responsibility.\" You can make comments, cite examples or use your personal experiences to develop your essay. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words. You should copy the sentence given in quotes at the beginning of your essay.", "sample": "In an era of rapid social development, it is vitally important to enhance young people's sense of responsibility.\nAs society changes at an unprecedented pace, young people often face distractions and pressures that weaken\ntheir commitment to family, community, and the future. Without a strong sense of responsibility, they may\nbecome self-centered and short-sighted.\nThe decline of responsibility among youth is partly caused by the instant gratification culture brought by\ntechnology and social media. Many young people are used to receiving immediate rewards and may avoid\nduties that require long-term effort. To address this problem, schools and families should work together to\ncultivate accountability through real tasks and role models. For example, involving students in community\nservice can help them understand the value of contributing to society.\nIn conclusion, enhancing young people's sense of responsibility is not only a personal virtue but also a social\nnecessity. Only when the younger generation takes responsibility seriously can society achieve sustainable\ndevelopment and harmony."}, "translation": {"prompt": "电子商务的蓬勃发展正在深刻改变中国农村的面貌。随着互联网和物流网络快速向农村延伸， 越来越多的农村居民通过电子商务平台将农产品直接销售给城市消费者。近年来，直播带货 ( live- streaming commerce) 使城市消费者能够与卖家实时互动，已成为促进农村发展的一种新方式。电子商务打破了城乡之间的信息壁垒，拓宽了农产品销售渠道，帮助农村居民增收致富。数字经济与乡村振兴的深度融合，正在为中国农村发展注入前所未有的活力。", "sample": "The booming development of e-commerce is profoundly changing the face of rural China. As the Internet and logistics networks rapidly extend into rural areas, more and more rural residents are selling their agricultural products directly to urban consumers through e-commerce platforms. In recent years, live-streaming commerce, which enables urban consumers to interact with sellers in real time, has become a new way to promote rural development. E-commerce has broken down the information barriers between urban and rural areas and expanded sales channels for agricultural products, helping rural residents increase their incomes and achieve prosperity. The deep integration of the digital economy with rural revitalization is injecting unprecedented vitality into China's rural development."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet6/paper/2026-06-2/?f=w", "clozePassage": "Most financial crises have plenty in common: they tend to start in the banking sector and involve excessive borrowing, together with an asset bubble. The  __26__  crisis of 2008 was no different, with the asset bubble focused on US real estate. But my research suggests this crisis had another  __27__  cause—that some people in the banking sector were playing or \"gaming\" the system for their own financial gain.\nThe game being played had several important features. The first was the deliberate complexity of the financial products at its core. Then there are the  __28__  biases that permeate decision-making at all levels of the banking industry. Banking often attracts people with certain personality  __29__  , including those who are overconfident and take excessive risks.\nSuch people tend to like complexity for its own sake, but they often do not fully understand the  __30__  of that complexity for the stability of the financial system as a whole. Often they do not care—they are primarily interested in gaming the system to  __31__  their bonuses.\nThe next element is risk. There are parts of the banking sector that will always be  __32__  to risk, but many bankers have come to feel immune to its potential impact, because they  __33__  that, however recklessly banks behave, governments will always be there to bail them out.\nGaming in the banking sector seems virtually impossible to  __34__  . The only effective measure to end it would be to make bankers  __35__  liable for losses, but no government has ever passed such a law, and no single government could do so on its own, since this would immediately cause their entire national banking sector to move wholesale to another jurisdiction.", "matchIntro": "How do you bring a forest back to life?", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "Five hundred years ago, forests covered much of the Iberian peninsula. But that soon changed. Centuries of wars and invasions, agricultural expansion and woodcutting for firewood and shipping wiped out most of the woods and transformed places like Matamorisca, a small village in northern Spain, into degraded landscapes."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The region's dry climate and depleted soils would be a recipe for disaster in your average program to replant trees, but for the Amsterdam-based Land Life Company it's an ideal place. \"We typically operate where nature does not come back by itself,\" says Jurrian Ruys, its CEO. \"We go where there are rougher conditions in terms of weather, with rough or very hot summers.\" In Matamorisca, they intervened in 17 barren hectares owned by the regional government and peppered them with their signature device: a biodegradable(可生物降解的) cardboard container they call the cocoon which can hold 25 litres of water underground to aid a seedling's first year. Around 16,000 trees, including oaks, ashes, walnuts, rowans, and whitebeams, were planted in May 2018, and the company reports that 96% of them survived that year's burning hot summer without extra irrigation, a critical milestone for a young tree."}, {"label": "C", "text": "\"Does nature return by itself?\" asks Arnout Asjes, Land Life Company's chief technology officer, who oversees a mix of drone(无人机) and satellite imagery, big data analysis, soil enhancement, QR(二维码) tagging and site-specific tree configuration designs. \"Probably, but it can take decades or hundreds of years, so we are speeding things up.\""}, {"label": "D", "text": "His company belongs to a global movement of organisations trying to save degraded or deforested areas, ranging from fertile tropical lowlands to dry hills in temperate(温带的) regions. Spurred by global biodiversity loss and climate change, these groups are pushing the boundary of how to revive forest cover. \"It's not a theoretical proposition,\" says Walter Vergara, a forest and climate specialist at the World Resources Institute(WRI). \"It requires the right incentives, the right stakeholders, the right analysis and sufficient capital, but it can happen.\""}, {"label": "E", "text": "How these factors come together for a particular project—and whether saving destroyed woods is even possible—depends on what kind of ecosystem you have in mind. Secondary forests in the Amazon are different from Texas pines recovering after wildfires or the Arctic timber woods that cover much of Sweden. Each provides different reasons for reforestation programs and each has particular needs. In the dry conditions around Matamorisca and similar areas in Spain, Land Life Company worries about rapid desertification(沙漠化). As its focus is on restoring an ecosystem, it works with organisations that don't expect their money back."}, {"label": "F", "text": "With around 600 hectares replanted around the world since 2015 and another 1,100 planned for this year, the company's drive fits into the Bonn Challenge, a global effort to restore 150 million hectares of the world's deforested and degraded land by 2020. That's an area roughly the size of Iran or Mongolia. By 2030, the goal is to reach 350 million hectares—20% more land than India. These targets include both the rehabilitation of forested areas that have lost density or look a bit weak (a process known as 'restoration' in forestry jargon) and the recovery of forest cover in areas where it was completely cleared (what's known as 'reforestation')."}, {"label": "G", "text": "That global target is broken into smaller pieces and takes shape in Latin America as the 20x20 Initiative, an effort to contribute 20 million hectares to the overall goal by initiating small to medium projects with the political backing of governments. Unlike Land Life Company, this region-wide project makes the economic and business case for reforestation, even though they are after biodiversity conservation. \"You need to bring in private sector money,\" says WRI's Vergara, who leads the initiative, \"and that capital needs to see a return on their investment.\" A study he led predicts Latin America would see an estimated net present value of about $23bn over a 50-year period if it reaches its goal."}, {"label": "H", "text": "Money can come from timber sales in sustainably managed forests or from harvesting 'non-wood products' like nuts, oils and fruits from the trees. You can take note of how much carbon dioxide your forest is capturing and sell carbon credits to companies keen to offset their emissions. Or you can even grow the forest hoping that biodiversity will attract ecotourists who'd pay for lodging, birdwatching tours and meals."}, {"label": "I", "text": "Still, these backers are not your high-street bank. Money for the 20x20 Initiative comes mostly from financial organisations with triple goal—a modest return on their investment, environmental benefits and social gains—known as impact investors. Take the German fund 12Tree for instance, one of the 20x20 partners. It put $9.5 million into Cuango, a 1,455-hectare property on the Caribbean coast of Panama that combines a commercial cocoa tree plantation with timber extraction from a sustainably managed secondary forest. With their money, they reforested a former cattle ranch, provided high-quality jobs for the surrounding communities and made a return on their investment."}, {"label": "J", "text": "Even on land cleared decades ago and currently used by farmers, some crops can coexist with the forest, if you find the right balance. Although not technically reforestation, agroforestry (a system of land use in which harvestable trees or shrubs are grown among or around crops or on pasture land, as a means of preserving or enhancing the productivity of the land) provides an opportunity for small-holder farmers to sustain their livelihoods while adding some forest cover to their farms."}, {"label": "K", "text": "A global project called Breedcafs is studying how trees behave in coffee farms, hoping to find crop varieties that manage to grow under the shade of trees. Coffee grows naturally in such forests, so copying that in farms is taking the crop back to its roots. \"By reintroducing trees in the landscape, we impact positively humidity, rain capture, soil conservation and biodiversity preservation,\" says coffee expert Benoit Bertrand, who leads the project from the French Agricultural Research Centre for International Development. Bertrand is analysing which of dozens of coffee varieties fits better for this system. A similar approach can be applied to land with cocoa, vanilla and fruit trees."}, {"label": "L", "text": "Not every piece of land is fit for reforestation. Vergara's partners look for safe investments, and even Land Life Company only runs major projects in what they consider \"low-risk\" countries. \"We tend to avoid large-scale operations in countries in some parts of the Middle East or Africa where the permanence is not secured,\" says Ruys."}, {"label": "M", "text": "But in the right location, perhaps all you need is time. In the Central Pacific of Costa Rica, the 330-hectare National Wildlife Refuge Baru looks nothing like the cattle ranch it was before 1987, when Jack Ewing decided to turn this large estate into an ecotourism destination. Instead of intervening, a friend told him to let nature have its way. Baru's former grazing lands are now thriving woods and the property boasts over 150 hectares of secondary forests with no intervention from humans. In the past 10 years, animals such as howling monkeys, scarlet macaws and even migratory cougars have returned to the refuge's land, boosting tourism and revitalising the ecosystem. Ewing, now 75, explains this success using the words his friend chose three decades ago: \"In Costa Rica, when you stop managing the shrubs, the jungle comes back for its revenge.\""}], "readingPassages": {"46": "What does self-discipline look like at work? Sometimes, it's the ability to resist temptation, such as distractions to avoid a particular task. Other times it's the ability to persist with a task when your energy and motivation seem low.\nWhat do you believe about the nature of your own self-discipline? Is it something that runs on internal energy that is spent with use? Or is it something that is bolstered as you apply it and persist?\nYour answer matters, as it determines how much self-discipline you experience. For years, research in psychology pointed to self-control as a resource that is depleted with use and needs to be restored through breaks, rest, or sleep. More recently, however, research points to the importance of mindset. If you view self-discipline as an expendable resource, your willpower tends to fade with use. If you believe that exercising your self-control strengthens and fuels it, that is more likely to be your experience as you resist temptation or push yourself to get something done that is not appealing.\nIf you don't already view self-discipline as a force that feeds itself, what might you do? Reflect on times that you had initial difficulty resisting temptation, getting started on something, or sticking with it, but pushed ahead and accomplished the task or met the goal. That's the kind of experience we're talking about with this particular belief about self-discipline, and you've already had such an experience, so you've shown that it's possible. When you feel your willpower and motivation fading, remind yourself of those previous experiences and imagine the same happening now.\nWhat if your experiences have always seemed to indicate that your self-control diminishes with use, and you need breaks to recharge? There's nothing wrong with that as long as you can afford to take those breaks. If not, remind yourself that not everyone experiences self-discipline as a depletable resource and that your experience might be different if you focus on gathering more energy at this point rather than focusing on how difficult it is to continue or the need for a break.\nAlso, if you know someone who is a role model of self-discipline, picture that person in your situation, persisting in the task. Regardless of how you do it, as you push forward to continue to accomplish progress, be sure to mentally reinforce yourself by taking pride in your ability to exert more willpower as you persist.\nThe benefits of exerting willpower are cumulative. Your self-discipline is akin to a muscle, and each time you resist temptation or persist in a task despite a lack of motivation, you strengthen that muscle, leading to less effort and resistance in similar instances in the future.", "47": "What does self-discipline look like at work? Sometimes, it's the ability to resist temptation, such as distractions to avoid a particular task. Other times it's the ability to persist with a task when your energy and motivation seem low.\nWhat do you believe about the nature of your own self-discipline? Is it something that runs on internal energy that is spent with use? Or is it something that is bolstered as you apply it and persist?\nYour answer matters, as it determines how much self-discipline you experience. For years, research in psychology pointed to self-control as a resource that is depleted with use and needs to be restored through breaks, rest, or sleep. More recently, however, research points to the importance of mindset. If you view self-discipline as an expendable resource, your willpower tends to fade with use. If you believe that exercising your self-control strengthens and fuels it, that is more likely to be your experience as you resist temptation or push yourself to get something done that is not appealing.\nIf you don't already view self-discipline as a force that feeds itself, what might you do? Reflect on times that you had initial difficulty resisting temptation, getting started on something, or sticking with it, but pushed ahead and accomplished the task or met the goal. That's the kind of experience we're talking about with this particular belief about self-discipline, and you've already had such an experience, so you've shown that it's possible. When you feel your willpower and motivation fading, remind yourself of those previous experiences and imagine the same happening now.\nWhat if your experiences have always seemed to indicate that your self-control diminishes with use, and you need breaks to recharge? There's nothing wrong with that as long as you can afford to take those breaks. If not, remind yourself that not everyone experiences self-discipline as a depletable resource and that your experience might be different if you focus on gathering more energy at this point rather than focusing on how difficult it is to continue or the need for a break.\nAlso, if you know someone who is a role model of self-discipline, picture that person in your situation, persisting in the task. Regardless of how you do it, as you push forward to continue to accomplish progress, be sure to mentally reinforce yourself by taking pride in your ability to exert more willpower as you persist.\nThe benefits of exerting willpower are cumulative. Your self-discipline is akin to a muscle, and each time you resist temptation or persist in a task despite a lack of motivation, you strengthen that muscle, leading to less effort and resistance in similar instances in the future.", "48": "What does self-discipline look like at work? Sometimes, it's the ability to resist temptation, such as distractions to avoid a particular task. Other times it's the ability to persist with a task when your energy and motivation seem low.\nWhat do you believe about the nature of your own self-discipline? Is it something that runs on internal energy that is spent with use? Or is it something that is bolstered as you apply it and persist?\nYour answer matters, as it determines how much self-discipline you experience. For years, research in psychology pointed to self-control as a resource that is depleted with use and needs to be restored through breaks, rest, or sleep. More recently, however, research points to the importance of mindset. If you view self-discipline as an expendable resource, your willpower tends to fade with use. If you believe that exercising your self-control strengthens and fuels it, that is more likely to be your experience as you resist temptation or push yourself to get something done that is not appealing.\nIf you don't already view self-discipline as a force that feeds itself, what might you do? Reflect on times that you had initial difficulty resisting temptation, getting started on something, or sticking with it, but pushed ahead and accomplished the task or met the goal. That's the kind of experience we're talking about with this particular belief about self-discipline, and you've already had such an experience, so you've shown that it's possible. When you feel your willpower and motivation fading, remind yourself of those previous experiences and imagine the same happening now.\nWhat if your experiences have always seemed to indicate that your self-control diminishes with use, and you need breaks to recharge? There's nothing wrong with that as long as you can afford to take those breaks. If not, remind yourself that not everyone experiences self-discipline as a depletable resource and that your experience might be different if you focus on gathering more energy at this point rather than focusing on how difficult it is to continue or the need for a break.\nAlso, if you know someone who is a role model of self-discipline, picture that person in your situation, persisting in the task. Regardless of how you do it, as you push forward to continue to accomplish progress, be sure to mentally reinforce yourself by taking pride in your ability to exert more willpower as you persist.\nThe benefits of exerting willpower are cumulative. Your self-discipline is akin to a muscle, and each time you resist temptation or persist in a task despite a lack of motivation, you strengthen that muscle, leading to less effort and resistance in similar instances in the future.", "49": "What does self-discipline look like at work? Sometimes, it's the ability to resist temptation, such as distractions to avoid a particular task. Other times it's the ability to persist with a task when your energy and motivation seem low.\nWhat do you believe about the nature of your own self-discipline? Is it something that runs on internal energy that is spent with use? Or is it something that is bolstered as you apply it and persist?\nYour answer matters, as it determines how much self-discipline you experience. For years, research in psychology pointed to self-control as a resource that is depleted with use and needs to be restored through breaks, rest, or sleep. More recently, however, research points to the importance of mindset. If you view self-discipline as an expendable resource, your willpower tends to fade with use. If you believe that exercising your self-control strengthens and fuels it, that is more likely to be your experience as you resist temptation or push yourself to get something done that is not appealing.\nIf you don't already view self-discipline as a force that feeds itself, what might you do? Reflect on times that you had initial difficulty resisting temptation, getting started on something, or sticking with it, but pushed ahead and accomplished the task or met the goal. That's the kind of experience we're talking about with this particular belief about self-discipline, and you've already had such an experience, so you've shown that it's possible. When you feel your willpower and motivation fading, remind yourself of those previous experiences and imagine the same happening now.\nWhat if your experiences have always seemed to indicate that your self-control diminishes with use, and you need breaks to recharge? There's nothing wrong with that as long as you can afford to take those breaks. If not, remind yourself that not everyone experiences self-discipline as a depletable resource and that your experience might be different if you focus on gathering more energy at this point rather than focusing on how difficult it is to continue or the need for a break.\nAlso, if you know someone who is a role model of self-discipline, picture that person in your situation, persisting in the task. Regardless of how you do it, as you push forward to continue to accomplish progress, be sure to mentally reinforce yourself by taking pride in your ability to exert more willpower as you persist.\nThe benefits of exerting willpower are cumulative. Your self-discipline is akin to a muscle, and each time you resist temptation or persist in a task despite a lack of motivation, you strengthen that muscle, leading to less effort and resistance in similar instances in the future.", "50": "What does self-discipline look like at work? Sometimes, it's the ability to resist temptation, such as distractions to avoid a particular task. Other times it's the ability to persist with a task when your energy and motivation seem low.\nWhat do you believe about the nature of your own self-discipline? Is it something that runs on internal energy that is spent with use? Or is it something that is bolstered as you apply it and persist?\nYour answer matters, as it determines how much self-discipline you experience. For years, research in psychology pointed to self-control as a resource that is depleted with use and needs to be restored through breaks, rest, or sleep. More recently, however, research points to the importance of mindset. If you view self-discipline as an expendable resource, your willpower tends to fade with use. If you believe that exercising your self-control strengthens and fuels it, that is more likely to be your experience as you resist temptation or push yourself to get something done that is not appealing.\nIf you don't already view self-discipline as a force that feeds itself, what might you do? Reflect on times that you had initial difficulty resisting temptation, getting started on something, or sticking with it, but pushed ahead and accomplished the task or met the goal. That's the kind of experience we're talking about with this particular belief about self-discipline, and you've already had such an experience, so you've shown that it's possible. When you feel your willpower and motivation fading, remind yourself of those previous experiences and imagine the same happening now.\nWhat if your experiences have always seemed to indicate that your self-control diminishes with use, and you need breaks to recharge? There's nothing wrong with that as long as you can afford to take those breaks. If not, remind yourself that not everyone experiences self-discipline as a depletable resource and that your experience might be different if you focus on gathering more energy at this point rather than focusing on how difficult it is to continue or the need for a break.\nAlso, if you know someone who is a role model of self-discipline, picture that person in your situation, persisting in the task. Regardless of how you do it, as you push forward to continue to accomplish progress, be sure to mentally reinforce yourself by taking pride in your ability to exert more willpower as you persist.\nThe benefits of exerting willpower are cumulative. Your self-discipline is akin to a muscle, and each time you resist temptation or persist in a task despite a lack of motivation, you strengthen that muscle, leading to less effort and resistance in similar instances in the future.", "51": "Smartphones have always posed challenges for parents of teens. From social media apps to excessive screen time, the digital world is as threatening as the physical.\nA new study shows that about half of parents of teens are worried about their teens being exposed to explicit content online. This is a valid concern, of course. But there are ways to alleviate the spread of explicit content, from changing the settings in their kids' phone to monitoring such content with apps like Bark.\nJust as alarming, if not more so, is that the amount of time teens spend on social media can lead to anxiety and depression.\nExcessive screen time has detrimental effects, for a lot of obvious reasons. Constant media consumption inhibits healthy activities associated with childhood, like spending time outside, exercising, talking to friends and family. If it interferes with sleep, that's even worse.\nAmple research shows too much smartphone use produces significant anxiety, depression, and feelings of isolation in teens. Teens log on to social media or spend time online trying to make connections, but end up feeling more alone and sad than ever. Faceless communication not only produces feelings of isolation and loneliness but increases instances of online bullying and susceptibility to criminal conducts.\nThough I've banned most social media, I've still battled smartphone time with my own kids. A lot of the time I wish I could throw my kids' smartphones into the nearest lake. The convenience of communication—when they need a ride home from school after practice, for instance—often feels like it isn't worth the battle to keep their screen time low and productive, and continue to encourage \"real-life\" activities outside and with friends and family. I understand the addiction and often tell them I'm not sure how I would have handled having a smartphone as a teen. As an adult, I too, struggle with overuse.\nIn a recent TV interview to promote a new film about a mother and daughter navigating social media use, actress Kate Winslet expressed her view on teens and social media—and her concern mirrors the trends. \"I do definitely feel that the world of social media is frightening to parents because we don't really know what's there,\" she said. \"We don't know how our children are using it. We don't know the effects that it's really having on them.\"\nI don't think the U.S. government should regulate social media and teen smartphone use, but it is a vital topic for parents to understand and take seriously. Unfortunately, now, parents don't just have to worry about real-world dangers, but all the ones sneaking online and the effects they have on teenagers and their mental health.", "52": "Smartphones have always posed challenges for parents of teens. From social media apps to excessive screen time, the digital world is as threatening as the physical.\nA new study shows that about half of parents of teens are worried about their teens being exposed to explicit content online. This is a valid concern, of course. But there are ways to alleviate the spread of explicit content, from changing the settings in their kids' phone to monitoring such content with apps like Bark.\nJust as alarming, if not more so, is that the amount of time teens spend on social media can lead to anxiety and depression.\nExcessive screen time has detrimental effects, for a lot of obvious reasons. Constant media consumption inhibits healthy activities associated with childhood, like spending time outside, exercising, talking to friends and family. If it interferes with sleep, that's even worse.\nAmple research shows too much smartphone use produces significant anxiety, depression, and feelings of isolation in teens. Teens log on to social media or spend time online trying to make connections, but end up feeling more alone and sad than ever. Faceless communication not only produces feelings of isolation and loneliness but increases instances of online bullying and susceptibility to criminal conducts.\nThough I've banned most social media, I've still battled smartphone time with my own kids. A lot of the time I wish I could throw my kids' smartphones into the nearest lake. The convenience of communication—when they need a ride home from school after practice, for instance—often feels like it isn't worth the battle to keep their screen time low and productive, and continue to encourage \"real-life\" activities outside and with friends and family. I understand the addiction and often tell them I'm not sure how I would have handled having a smartphone as a teen. As an adult, I too, struggle with overuse.\nIn a recent TV interview to promote a new film about a mother and daughter navigating social media use, actress Kate Winslet expressed her view on teens and social media—and her concern mirrors the trends. \"I do definitely feel that the world of social media is frightening to parents because we don't really know what's there,\" she said. \"We don't know how our children are using it. We don't know the effects that it's really having on them.\"\nI don't think the U.S. government should regulate social media and teen smartphone use, but it is a vital topic for parents to understand and take seriously. Unfortunately, now, parents don't just have to worry about real-world dangers, but all the ones sneaking online and the effects they have on teenagers and their mental health.", "53": "Smartphones have always posed challenges for parents of teens. From social media apps to excessive screen time, the digital world is as threatening as the physical.\nA new study shows that about half of parents of teens are worried about their teens being exposed to explicit content online. This is a valid concern, of course. But there are ways to alleviate the spread of explicit content, from changing the settings in their kids' phone to monitoring such content with apps like Bark.\nJust as alarming, if not more so, is that the amount of time teens spend on social media can lead to anxiety and depression.\nExcessive screen time has detrimental effects, for a lot of obvious reasons. Constant media consumption inhibits healthy activities associated with childhood, like spending time outside, exercising, talking to friends and family. If it interferes with sleep, that's even worse.\nAmple research shows too much smartphone use produces significant anxiety, depression, and feelings of isolation in teens. Teens log on to social media or spend time online trying to make connections, but end up feeling more alone and sad than ever. Faceless communication not only produces feelings of isolation and loneliness but increases instances of online bullying and susceptibility to criminal conducts.\nThough I've banned most social media, I've still battled smartphone time with my own kids. A lot of the time I wish I could throw my kids' smartphones into the nearest lake. The convenience of communication—when they need a ride home from school after practice, for instance—often feels like it isn't worth the battle to keep their screen time low and productive, and continue to encourage \"real-life\" activities outside and with friends and family. I understand the addiction and often tell them I'm not sure how I would have handled having a smartphone as a teen. As an adult, I too, struggle with overuse.\nIn a recent TV interview to promote a new film about a mother and daughter navigating social media use, actress Kate Winslet expressed her view on teens and social media—and her concern mirrors the trends. \"I do definitely feel that the world of social media is frightening to parents because we don't really know what's there,\" she said. \"We don't know how our children are using it. We don't know the effects that it's really having on them.\"\nI don't think the U.S. government should regulate social media and teen smartphone use, but it is a vital topic for parents to understand and take seriously. Unfortunately, now, parents don't just have to worry about real-world dangers, but all the ones sneaking online and the effects they have on teenagers and their mental health.", "54": "Smartphones have always posed challenges for parents of teens. From social media apps to excessive screen time, the digital world is as threatening as the physical.\nA new study shows that about half of parents of teens are worried about their teens being exposed to explicit content online. This is a valid concern, of course. But there are ways to alleviate the spread of explicit content, from changing the settings in their kids' phone to monitoring such content with apps like Bark.\nJust as alarming, if not more so, is that the amount of time teens spend on social media can lead to anxiety and depression.\nExcessive screen time has detrimental effects, for a lot of obvious reasons. Constant media consumption inhibits healthy activities associated with childhood, like spending time outside, exercising, talking to friends and family. If it interferes with sleep, that's even worse.\nAmple research shows too much smartphone use produces significant anxiety, depression, and feelings of isolation in teens. Teens log on to social media or spend time online trying to make connections, but end up feeling more alone and sad than ever. Faceless communication not only produces feelings of isolation and loneliness but increases instances of online bullying and susceptibility to criminal conducts.\nThough I've banned most social media, I've still battled smartphone time with my own kids. A lot of the time I wish I could throw my kids' smartphones into the nearest lake. The convenience of communication—when they need a ride home from school after practice, for instance—often feels like it isn't worth the battle to keep their screen time low and productive, and continue to encourage \"real-life\" activities outside and with friends and family. I understand the addiction and often tell them I'm not sure how I would have handled having a smartphone as a teen. As an adult, I too, struggle with overuse.\nIn a recent TV interview to promote a new film about a mother and daughter navigating social media use, actress Kate Winslet expressed her view on teens and social media—and her concern mirrors the trends. \"I do definitely feel that the world of social media is frightening to parents because we don't really know what's there,\" she said. \"We don't know how our children are using it. We don't know the effects that it's really having on them.\"\nI don't think the U.S. government should regulate social media and teen smartphone use, but it is a vital topic for parents to understand and take seriously. Unfortunately, now, parents don't just have to worry about real-world dangers, but all the ones sneaking online and the effects they have on teenagers and their mental health.", "55": "Smartphones have always posed challenges for parents of teens. From social media apps to excessive screen time, the digital world is as threatening as the physical.\nA new study shows that about half of parents of teens are worried about their teens being exposed to explicit content online. This is a valid concern, of course. But there are ways to alleviate the spread of explicit content, from changing the settings in their kids' phone to monitoring such content with apps like Bark.\nJust as alarming, if not more so, is that the amount of time teens spend on social media can lead to anxiety and depression.\nExcessive screen time has detrimental effects, for a lot of obvious reasons. Constant media consumption inhibits healthy activities associated with childhood, like spending time outside, exercising, talking to friends and family. If it interferes with sleep, that's even worse.\nAmple research shows too much smartphone use produces significant anxiety, depression, and feelings of isolation in teens. Teens log on to social media or spend time online trying to make connections, but end up feeling more alone and sad than ever. Faceless communication not only produces feelings of isolation and loneliness but increases instances of online bullying and susceptibility to criminal conducts.\nThough I've banned most social media, I've still battled smartphone time with my own kids. A lot of the time I wish I could throw my kids' smartphones into the nearest lake. The convenience of communication—when they need a ride home from school after practice, for instance—often feels like it isn't worth the battle to keep their screen time low and productive, and continue to encourage \"real-life\" activities outside and with friends and family. I understand the addiction and often tell them I'm not sure how I would have handled having a smartphone as a teen. As an adult, I too, struggle with overuse.\nIn a recent TV interview to promote a new film about a mother and daughter navigating social media use, actress Kate Winslet expressed her view on teens and social media—and her concern mirrors the trends. \"I do definitely feel that the world of social media is frightening to parents because we don't really know what's there,\" she said. \"We don't know how our children are using it. We don't know the effects that it's really having on them.\"\nI don't think the U.S. government should regulate social media and teen smartphone use, but it is a vital topic for parents to understand and take seriously. Unfortunately, now, parents don't just have to worry about real-world dangers, but all the ones sneaking online and the effects they have on teenagers and their mental health."}, "questions": [{"id": "2026-06-2-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What is the man planning to do?", "options": ["A) Celebrate his 10th wedding anniversary.", "B) Celebrate his promotion in Barcelona.", "C) Give his wife a pleasant surprise.", "D) Ask for leave to visit Barcelona."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Celebrate his 10th wedding anniversary.\n【定位】It's my 10th wedding anniversary next week, and I promised Jane that I would book\na city break for us, as I have annual leave to take, and it would be nice to celebrate\nsomewhere warm.\n下周是我结婚十周年纪念日，我答应 Jane 会给我们订一次城市短途旅行，因为我有年假要\n休，而且去个暖和的地方庆祝会很好。\n【信号】承诺句+原因铺陈。男士用 I promised Jane that I would book a city break for us 直接\n说明自己的计划，后面 as 引出原因；这是典型的'被问者应答'，直接承载答案。\n【替换】It's my 10th wedding anniversary next week ↔ Celebrate his 10th wedding anniversary.\n事件同义概括\nI promised Jane that I would book a city break for us ↔ Celebrate his 10th wedding anniversary.\n计划句→事件目的\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nCelebrate his promotion in Barcelona. 盗用了原文 in Barcelona，但录音说的是去\nBarcelona 庆祝周年纪念，而非庆祝升职，promotion 未提及。\n借⽤原⽂：in Barcelona\nC) 过度推断\nGive his wife a pleasant surprise. 把原文 I promised Jane 的浪漫氛围曲解为 surprise，\n录音中男士是答应订旅行，并未说是惊喜。\nD) 原词陷阱\nAsk for leave to visit Barcelona. 盗用原文 as I have annual leave to take，但录音说'有\n年假要休'，并非'申请请假'，动作被偷换。\n借⽤原⽂：as I have annual leave to take", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "Why is the man starting to feel a bit frantic?", "options": ["A) He is unable to find some inexpensive accommodation.", "B) There are few warm places to go at this time of the year.", "C) There is so much for him to do before going on vacation.", "D) He is afraid Barcelona may not be so fabulous as expected."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） He is unable to find some inexpensive accommodation.\n【定位】But the anniversary is looming, and I'm still scrambling to find accommodation. Everything is\neither wildly expensive or already fully booked. I'm starting to feel a bit frantic.\n但纪念日越来越近，我还在拼命找住宿。要么贵得离谱，要么已经订满。我开始有点慌了。\n【信号】问题铺陈+情绪词。I'm still scrambling to find accommodation 点明困难，Everything\nis either wildly expensive or already fully booked 具体说明，I'm starting to feel a bit\nfrantic 直接回应题干情绪。\n【替换】I'm still scrambling to find accommodation ↔\nHe is unable to find some inexpensive accommodation.\n动作+结果同义改写\nEverything is either wildly expensive or already fully booked ↔\nHe is unable to find some inexpensive accommodation.\n反向同义替换\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nThere are few warm places to go at this time of the year. 盗用原文 somewhere\nwarm，但原文说想找个暖和地方庆祝，没说暖和地方少，属原词陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：somewhere warm\nC) 过度推断\nThere is so much for him to do before going on vacation. 把找住宿的困难扩大为'很多\n事要做'，录音未提及其他事务，属过度推断。\nD) 与原文相反\nHe is afraid Barcelona may not be so fabulous as expected. 与女士说的 fabulous\ncosmopolitan city 方向相反，且男士慌乱原因是住宿而非担心城市不好。\n借⽤原⽂：fabulous cosmopolitan city", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What does the woman suggest the man do?", "options": ["A) Rent a car to drive into the city and scenic spots so as to save the expenses on transportation.", "B) Spend a couple of nights in a glamorous hotel and the rest of the week in inexpensive places.", "C) Find all-inclusive accommodation to eliminate the need to pay for food and drinks.", "D) Stay in a hotel away from the coast where the accommodation is less expensive."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\nSpend a couple of nights in a glamorous hotel and the rest of the week in inexpensive places.\n【定位】Well, if you are going for a whole week, why don't you do a couple of nights in somewhere\nreally glamorous, and then do the rest of the week a bit further up the coast, where the\naccommodation is cheaper, and that will offset the cost of the expensive place?\n嗯，如果你要去一整周，为什么不先找家很华丽的地方住两晚，然后剩下的时间住到海岸更\n北一点的地方，那里住宿更便宜，可以抵消贵地方的费用。\n【信号】建议句型 why don't you。why don't you do a couple of nights in somewhere really\nglamorous 是典型的建议句，且该句包含具体方案（住两晚贵的+其余住便宜的），直接对\n应题干。\n【替换】do a couple of nights in somewhere really glamorous ↔\nSpend a couple of nights in a glamorous hotel and the rest of the week in inexpensive places.\n同义替换：地点下位化+动词替换\ndo the rest of the week a bit further up the coast, where the accommodation is cheaper ↔\nSpend a couple of nights in a glamorous hotel and the rest of the week in inexpensive places.\n同义替换： cheaper accommodation = inexpensive places\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\nRent a car to drive into the city and scenic spots so as to save the expenses on\ntransportation. 盗用原文 It's not too expensive to rent a car，但这是男士对建议的回\n应，不是女士原建议，属张冠李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：It's not too expensive to rent a car\nC) 张冠李戴\nFind all-inclusive accommodation to eliminate the need to pay for food and drinks.\n盗用原文 all inclusive, so we wouldn't even need to pay for food or drinks，但这是男\n士想到的后续方案，并非女士最初建议。\n借⽤原⽂：all inclusive, so we wouldn't even need to pay for food or drinks\nD) 偷换概念\nStay in a hotel away from the coast where the accommodation is less expensive. 盗\n用原文 a bit further up the coast，但原文是'沿海岸更往北'，不是'远离海岸'，方向被改\n动。\n借⽤原⽂：a bit further up the coast", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What does the man say about his wife Jane?", "options": ["A) She is sure to like the cathedral.", "B) She is a romantic at heart.", "C) She is no good at logistics.", "D) She is scared of heights."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） She is scared of heights.\n【定位】Considering Jane is terrified of heights, I think if I tried to make her do that, I would come\nback divorced.\n考虑到 Jane 恐高，我觉得如果我硬让她上去，回来就得离婚了。\n【信号】人物评价形容词 terrified。Jane is terrified of heights 直接给出 Jane 的特征，是题干所\n问的答案句。\n【替换】Jane is terrified of heights ↔ She is scared of heights.\n近义形容词替换\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\nShe is sure to like the cathedral. 盗用女士推荐 I really do recommend visiting that\ncathedral，但这是女士对景点的推荐，不是男士对妻子的评价，且男士暗示妻子不会爬上\n去。\n借⽤原⽂：I really do recommend visiting that cathedral\nB) 张冠李戴\nShe is a romantic at heart. 盗用原文 That sounds so romantic，但这是女士评价男士计\n划浪漫，不是说 Jane 浪漫。\n借⽤原⽂：That sounds so romantic\nC) 偷换概念\nShe is no good at logistics. 盗用原文 logistical nightmare，但 nightmare 说的是订行\n程麻烦，不是说 Jane 不擅长 logistics。\n借⽤原⽂：logistical nightmare", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What do we learn about the speakers?", "options": ["A) They often share each other's experiences.", "B) They enjoy interacting with patients.", "C) They always do things tricky.", "D) They are both pharmacists."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They are both pharmacists.\n【定位】Hi, Suzanne. Just keeping busy with the usual stuff, filling prescriptions, talking to patients\nabout their medications, that kind of thing.\n嗨，苏珊娜。就是忙着平常的活儿，配药、跟病人聊他们的用药情况，诸如此类的事。\n【信号】工作内容即身份。男性说话者在开场问候后直接描述日常工作：filling prescriptions,\ntalking to patients about their medications，这些正是药剂师的典型职责，从而泄露两\n人身份。\n【替换】filling prescriptions, talking to patients about their medications ↔ They are both pharmacists\n下位职责→上位职业\n【排除】A) 过度推断\n对话中两人确实交流了工作情况，但 They often share each other's experiences 中的\noften 和 share each other's experiences 都是过度概括，录音并未支持。\nB) 偷换概念\n录音提到 talking to patients about their medications，但那是工作内容，并未说 enjoy\ninteracting with patients，选项把行为偷换成情感态度。\nC) 原词陷阱\nThey always do things tricky 盗用了录音中的 tricky 原词，但原文 that's always tricky\n指的是处理药物相互作用这件事棘手，不是说说话者做事狡猾或爱耍花招。\n借⽤原⽂：that's always tricky", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What does the woman say about an interesting case she had?", "options": ["A) The doctor ended up admitting his mistake.", "B) The patient consulted another doctor.", "C) The patient actually took her advice.", "D) The doctor double-checked with her."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） The patient actually took her advice.\n【定位】I explained the issue to her as best I could, and recommended she double-check with her\ndoctor. And you know what? She actually followed my advice, and the doctor ended up\nswitching the patient's medication.\n我尽可能向她解释了这个问题，并建议她和医生再确认一下。你知道吗？她真的听从了我的\n建议，医生最终更换了病人的药物。\n【信号】强调词后出结果。女士先说建议内容，然后用 And you know what? 引出关键结果，再用\nactually 强调 She actually followed my advice，这是答案落点。\n【替换】She actually followed my advice ↔ The patient actually took her advice\n同义替换+代词还原\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nThe doctor ended up admitting his mistake 盗用了录音中的 the doctor ended up\nswitching the patient's medication，但把 switching the medication 偷换成了\nadmitting his mistake，内容完全改变。\n借⽤原⽂：the doctor ended up switching the patient's medication\nB) 偷换概念\n录音说 recommended she double-check with her doctor，是病人和自己的医生确认，\n不是咨询另一位医生，选项添加了 another。\nD) 张冠李戴\n录音是女士建议病人 double-check with her doctor，即病人去和医生确认；选项 The\ndoctor double-checked with her 把动作主体换成了 doctor，对象换成了女士，主谓宾关\n系完全颠倒。\n借⽤原⽂：recommended she double-check with her doctor", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "What does the man say he has been working on?", "options": ["A) A proposal for applying flu shots more effectively.", "B) A new medication therapy management program.", "C) A novel strategy to persuade stubborn patients.", "D) A therapy for patients with similar conditions."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） A new medication therapy management program.\n【定位】Good, good. I've been working on a new medication therapy management program for our\npatients with chronic conditions. It helps patients manage their medications more effectively.\n好，好。我一直在为我们的慢性病病人开发一个新的药物治疗管理项目。它帮助病人更有效\n地管理他们的药物。\n【信号】题干关键词复现。题干中的 has been working on 与录音中的 I've been working on 直接\n对应，这是典型的问答同构定位信号，紧跟其后的宾语就是答案。\n【替换】I've been working on a new medication therapy management program ↔\nA new medication therapy management program\n直接对应，去掉主谓\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA proposal for applying flu shots more effectively 盗用了录音 get their flu shots 中的\nflu shots，但那是女士提到的人流疫苗接种话题，与男士 working on 的项目无关。\n借⽤原⽂：get their flu shots\nC) 原词陷阱\nA novel strategy to persuade stubborn patients 盗用了录音 the patient is too\nstubborn and they refuse to listen to us 中的 stubborn，但那是两人感叹病人难劝，不\n是男士正在做的项目。\n借⽤原⽂：the patient is too stubborn and they refuse to listen to us\nD) 偷换概念\n录音说 patients with chronic conditions（慢性病患者），选项 A therapy for patients\nwith similar conditions 把 chronic 偷换成 similar，概念被替换。\n借⽤原⽂：patients with chronic conditions", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "What is a big problem, especially for older patients according to the woman?", "options": ["A) Inability to send text messages.", "B) Distrust in pharmaceutical experts.", "C) Medication non-adherence.", "D) Need for constant reminding."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Medication non-adherence.\n【定位】Medication non-adherence is a big problem, especially for older patients.\n用药不依从是一个大问题，尤其对老年患者来说。\n【信号】判断句与题干一一对应。女士直接说 Medication non-adherence is a big problem,\nespecially for older patients，与题干 What is a big problem, especially for older\npatients according to the woman? 几乎逐词对应。\n【替换】Medication non-adherence is a big problem, especially for older patients ↔\nMedication non-adherence\n原词复现\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nInability to send text messages 盗用了录音 text message reminders 中的 text\nmessages，但那是解决用药依从性的提醒手段，不是大问题本身。\n借⽤原⽂：Do you use text message reminders?\nB) 未提及\n录音中没有提到患者或公众对 pharmaceutical experts 的不信任，选项属于无中生有。\nD) 答非所问\n录音提到 text message reminders 是为了解决 medication non-adherence，Need for\nconstant reminding 把提醒需求当作大问题，但女士明确说的大问题是 Medication non-\nadherence。\n借⽤原⽂：Do you use text message reminders?", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What are passionate people considered likely to do in America?", "options": ["A) Accomplish their goals.", "B) View things positively.", "C) Overstate their importance.", "D) Dominate job interviews."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Accomplish their goals.\n【定位】On the whole, passionate people are thought of in a positive light and considered likely to\nachieve their goals.\n总体而言，有热情的人被认为形象正面，且被认为很可能实现他们的目标。\n【信号】直接描述句。passionate people are thought of in a positive light and considered\nlikely to achieve their goals是对passionate people的直接定义式描述，题干中的\n『considered likely to』几乎原文复现了该句结构。\n【替换】achieve their goals ↔ Accomplish their goals.\n同义词替换\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n录音确实提到positive light，但题干问的是『considered likely to do』（做什么），不是\n『被认为怎么样』（态度如何）；选项B把positive换个说法变成positively，看似用了原\n词，实则答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：passionate people are thought of in a positive light\nC) 偷换概念\n录音末尾有has the importance of passion been overstated?，但这是作者抛出的质疑问\n题，并非passionate people『夸大自己的重要性』；选项C把『热情的重要性被高估』偷\n换成『他们夸大自己的重要性』，主语和对象都错了。\n借⽤原⽂：has the importance of passion been overstated?\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音开头提到in interviews，但只说明passion这个词常在面试中出现，并未说passionate\npeople『主导面试』；『dominate』是无中生有，利用『job interviews』这个原词制造\n『听到就选』的诱惑。\n借⽤原⽂：in job descriptions and in interviews", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "What does a new study from Stanford show about the importance of passion in predicting achievement?", "options": ["A) It reflects a strong sense of obligation to others.", "B) It signifies a universal model of motivation.", "C) It may differ in various cultures.", "D) It may have been underestimated."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It may differ in various cultures.\n【定位】According to a new study from Stanford University, passion may be less important in certain\ncultures, and the fact that passion is often seen as a key to achievement may reflect a\ndistinctly Western model of motivation.\n根据斯坦福大学的一项新研究，热情在某些文化中可能不那么重要，而且热情常被视为成就\n关键这一事实，可能反映的是一种明显西方化的动机模式。\n【信号】研究来源+结论句。According to a new study from Stanford University是典型的研究结\n论引导语，其后『passion may be less important in certain cultures』直接回应题干中\n『the importance of passion in predicting achievement』。\n【替换】passion may be less important in certain cultures ↔ It may differ in various cultures.\n具体→概括\nreflect a distinctly Western model of motivation ↔ It may differ in various cultures.\n西方模式≠普世模式\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\n录音确实提到fulfilling obligations to others，但那是用来描述『collectivistic cultures』\n中人们更看重的东西，不是关于『passion在预测成就中的重要性』的结论；选项A把集体主\n义文化的特征张冠李戴到研究主题上。\n借⽤原⽂：They often place fulfilling obligations to others above focusing on their own interests.\nB) 与原文相反\n录音明确说热情作为成就关键反映的是『a distinctly Western model of motivation』，\n即只是西方模式而非普世模式；选项B的『universal model』与原文直接相反。\n借⽤原⽂：reflect a distinctly Western model of motivation\nD) 偷换概念\n录音说热情在某些文化中『less important』，这是说被重视/被强调得没那么高，不等同于\n『被低估（underestimated）』；『低估』暗含实际应更高，而原文只说相对重要性较\n低，方向不同。\n借⽤原⽂：passion may be less important in certain cultures", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What did the researchers find about the effect of passion on academic achievement for those in more collectivistic societies?", "options": ["A) It was under-investigated.", "B) It was more predictable.", "C) It was taken for granted.", "D) It was more subtle."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It was more subtle.\n【定位】The effect of passion on performance was more subtle for those in more collectivistic\nsocieties such as Colombia or Thailand.\n对于那些更具集体主义文化的社会（如哥伦比亚或泰国）的人来说，热情对表现的影响更为\n微妙。\n【信号】对比结论句。The effect of passion on performance was more subtle for those in\nmore collectivistic societies直接回应题干中的『the effect of passion on academic\nachievement for those in more collectivistic societies』，『more subtle』是结论\n落点。\n【替换】The effect of passion on performance was more subtle for those in more collectivistic societies ↔\nIt was more subtle.\n同义复现\n【排除】A) 未提及\n录音从未说热情对集体主义社会的影响『研究不足（under-investigated）』；原文提到研\n究覆盖近60个国家，反而说明调查范围广，选项A无中生有。\nB) 未提及\n录音说影响『more subtle』，意味着更难察觉、更不明显，反而说明不太predictable；选\n项B的『more predictable』既无原文依据，也与subtle的隐含方向相悖。\nC) 未提及\n录音没有提到任何人把热情对学业成就的影响『视为理所当然（taken for granted）』，这\n是利用考生常识编造的干扰。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "What does the passage say is a pattern in nature?", "options": ["A) It is any incidental happening in plants and animals or ice crystals.", "B) It is any basic physical or chemical work in designing substances.", "C) It is any symmetrical and colorful stripes or spots on organisms.", "D) It is any regularly repeated arrangement of shapes or colors."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It is any regularly repeated arrangement of shapes or colors.\n【定位】A pattern in nature is any regularly repeated arrangement of shapes or colors.\n自然界中的图案是指形状或颜色的任何有规律重复排列。\n【信号】定义句直接作答。句中 A pattern in nature is 是典型的下定义句式，题干问 what is a\npattern，答案直接落在 is 后的表语。\n【替换】any regularly repeated arrangement of shapes or colors ↔\nany regularly repeated arrangement of shapes or colors\n原词复现\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\n选项 incidental happening 把原文 regularly repeated 偷换成 incidental，将'规律性'改\n为'偶然性'，与定义相反。\n借⽤原⽂：regularly repeated arrangement\nB) 原词陷阱\n选项 basic physical or chemical work 盗用第一段 basic physical or chemical processes\n的原词，但把 processes 改为 work，把对象改为 designing substances，答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：basic physical or chemical processes\nC)\n选项 symmetrical and colorful stripes or spots 对应第二段 colorful stripes and spots\n中的例子，但题干问的是定义而非例子。\n借⽤原⽂：colorful stripes and spots", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "What gives rise to complex patterns at a larger scale?", "options": ["A) Materials undergoing certain processes.", "B) Materials exposed to various natural forces.", "C) Materials impacted by any change of molecules.", "D) Materials going through extremely radical transformations."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Materials undergoing certain processes.\n【定位】Patterns like these begin to form at a small scale when materials undergo processes like\ndrying, freezing, wrinkling, diffusing and reacting. Those changes then give rise to complex\npatterns at a larger scale that people can see.\n这类图案在小尺度上开始形成，当材料经历干燥、冻结、起皱、扩散和反应等过程时。这些\n变化随后产生了人们能看到的大尺度复杂图案。\n【信号】过程链中的因果承接。前句讲 materials undergo processes，后句用 Those changes\nthen give rise to 承接，说明正是这些过程/变化导致了大尺度复杂图案。\n【替换】materials undergo processes like drying, freezing, wrinkling, diffusing and reacting ↔\nMaterials undergoing certain processes\n概括性同义替换\nThose changes then give rise to complex patterns at a larger scale ↔\nMaterials undergoing certain processes\n因果对应\n【排除】B) 张冠李戴\n选项 exposed to various natural forces 看似与第三段 many outside factors, including\nhumidity and temperature 有关，但题干问的是'大尺度复杂图案'的成因，而 outside\nfactors 是影响冰晶外形的外部条件，不是第二段讲的复杂图案形成机制。\n借⽤原⽂：many outside factors, including humidity and temperature\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项 change of molecules 把第一段 atoms and molecules 的微观层面与第二段的过程混\n为一谈，原文说的是复杂图案源于材料经历的过程，而非泛泛的分子变化。\n借⽤原⽂：atoms and molecules\nD) 偷换概念\n选项 extremely radical transformations 用 extremely radical 夸大了原文 processes like\ndrying, freezing, wrinkling, diffusing and reacting 的程度，原文只是说'某些过程'，并未\n强调'剧烈转变'。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What does the example of frozen crystals on a window show?", "options": ["A) The beauty of molecular clustering.", "B) The way for appreciating patterns.", "C) The creation of patterns.", "D) The factors affecting clusters."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） The creation of patterns.\n【定位】Imagine delicate frozen crystals on a window during winter. What creates that pattern? When\nwater freezes, its molecules begin clustering together. Water molecules have a particular bent\nshape that causes them to stack into clusters as they freeze. As the cluster grows, many\noutside factors, including humidity and temperature, begin to affect its overall shape. If the\nwater is freezing on a window, for example, random imperfections on the glass surface affect\nthe stacking and create the larger pattern.\n想象冬天窗户上精致的冰晶。是什么创造了那种图案？水结冰时，其分子开始聚集。水分子\n具有特殊的弯曲形状，使它们在结冰时堆叠成簇。随着簇的生长，包括湿度和温度在内的许\n多外部因素开始影响其整体形状。例如，如果水在窗户上结冰，玻璃表面的随机缺陷会影响\n堆叠并创造出更大的图案。\n【信号】设问句引出例子主旨。段落以 What creates that pattern? 设问，随后整段解释水分子\n聚集、外部因素、玻璃缺陷如何共同 create the larger pattern，说明例子服务于'图案的\n创造'。\n【替换】What creates that pattern? ↔ The creation of patterns\n同义概括\ncreate the larger pattern ↔ The creation of patterns\n首尾呼应\n【排除】A)\n选项 The beauty of molecular clustering 只涉及 molecules begin clustering together\n的局部细节，但例子并非为了展示'美'，而是为了解释图案如何形成。\n借⽤原⽂：molecules begin clustering together\nB) 未提及\n选项 The way for appreciating patterns 中的 appreciating 在原文中从未出现，属于脱离\n文本的主观臆断。\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 The factors affecting clusters 只对应 many outside factors, including humidity\nand temperature 这一部分，但例子还讲了分子聚集、玻璃缺陷等，且核心问题是 creates\nthat pattern，不是单纯讲影响因素。\n借⽤原⽂：many outside factors, including humidity and temperature", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What might molecular processes produce, according to the passage?", "options": ["A) A pattern by accident.", "B) Patterns for specific purposes.", "C) Patterns with random imperfections.", "D) A pattern with artificial intervention."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （推断题） A pattern by accident.\n【定位】But scientists do not always know the purpose of a pattern, or even if there is one. The\nmolecular processes involved are simple enough that they might generate a pattern\nby coincidence.\n但科学家并不总是知道某种图案的目的，甚至不知道它是否有目的。所涉及的分子过程足够\n简单，以至于它们可能偶然产生一种图案。\n【信号】转折后给可能性结论。But scientists do not always know the purpose of a pattern, or\neven if there is one 先让步，随后 might generate a pattern by coincidence 用 might\n给出可能性结论。\n【替换】generate a pattern by coincidence ↔ A pattern by accident\n同义替换\n【排除】B) 与原文相反\n选项 Patterns for specific purposes 与原文 scientists do not always know the purpose\nof a pattern, or even if there is one 相反，原文强调不一定有目的，选项却说'特定目的'。\n借⽤原⽂：scientists do not always know the purpose of a pattern, or even if there is one\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项 Patterns with random imperfections 盗用第三段 random imperfections on the\nglass surface 的原词，但那是窗户冰晶例子中的细节，与第四段分子过程可能产生的结果\n无关。\n借⽤原⽂：random imperfections on the glass surface\nD) 未提及\n选项 A pattern with artificial intervention 中的 artificial 在原文未提及，原文讨论的是自\n然过程中的分子机制，没有涉及人为干预。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "推断题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What does the speaker say is really difficult?", "options": ["A) Eliminating all the old habits at once.", "B) Making a permanent lifestyle change.", "C) Realizing all the life goals one has set.", "D) Improving multiple areas of one's life."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Making a permanent lifestyle change.\n【定位】The problem is, even if we are committed to working hard on our goals, our natural\ntendency is to return to our old habits at some point. Making a permanent lifestyle change is\nreally difficult.\n问题是，即使我们致力于努力实现目标，我们的自然倾向还是在某个时刻回到旧习惯。永久\n改变生活方式真的很难。\n【信号】题眼词复现 + 总结句。题干中的 difficult 在 Making a permanent lifestyle change is\nreally difficult 中直接复现；这是段落末尾的总结句，前面用 The problem is 铺垫，信息\n落点在 really difficult 上。\n【替换】Making a permanent lifestyle change is really difficult ↔ Making a permanent lifestyle change\n同义复现\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nEliminating all the old habits at once 盗用了 return to our old habits 里的 old\nhabits，但原文说的是“回到旧习惯”，不是“一次性根除旧习惯”，且 at once 原文未提。\n借⽤原⽂：return to our old habits\nC) 偷换概念\nRealizing all the life goals one has set 把 working hard on our goals 和 the goals I\nfind desirable 改成“实现所有既定目标”，但原文只说“努力追求目标”，并未说实现所有目\n标，all 也过于绝对。\n借⽤原⽂：the goals I find desirable\nD) 答非所问\nImproving multiple areas of one's life 在 multiple areas of life they would like to\nimprove 中确实出现，但那是 speaker 列举的“人们想改善的方面”，不是题干问的“really\ndifficult”的事。\n借⽤原⽂：multiple areas of life they would like to improve", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "What did researchers find about people with implementation intentions?", "options": ["A) They were more likely to implement a particular behavior.", "B) They were more likely to make plans for their future.", "C) They tended to be committed to a single goal.", "D) They tended to focus on one thing at a time."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） They were more likely to implement a particular behavior.\n【定位】Researchers found that people who filled out this sentence were two to three times more\nlikely to actually exercise compared to a control group who did not make plans for their\nfuture behavior.\n研究人员发现，填写了这句话的人真正去锻炼的可能性是对照组的两到三倍，而对照组没有\n为他们未来的行为制定计划。\n【信号】研究结论标志词。题干问 researchers find，原文 Researchers found that 正是结论信\n号；其后 two to three times more likely to actually exercise 给出量化结果。\n【替换】two to three times more likely to actually exercise ↔\nmore likely to implement a particular behavior\n具体实例概括为行为实施\nhow you intend to implement a particular behavior ↔ implement a particular behavior\n词组复现\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nmore likely to make plans for their future 盗用了 did not make plans for their future\nbehavior 里的 make plans for their future，但原文说对照组“没有”做计划，而实验组是\n“已经做了计划”并被发现有计划后更可能去锻炼，不是“更可能去做计划”。\n借⽤原⽂：did not make plans for their future behavior\nC) 张冠李戴\ntended to be committed to a single goal 把 focused on a single goal 从“专注单一目标\n的人更容易成功”移花接木到“有 implementation intentions 的人”，而题干问的是\nimplementation intentions 组，不是单一目标组。\n借⽤原⽂：focused on a single goal\nD) 偷换概念\nfocus on one thing at a time 来自 focus on one thing at a time，但这是 follow-up\nresearch 关于“单一目标”的限制条件，不是 researchers 对 implementation intentions 组\n的发现。\n借⽤原⽂：focus on one thing at a time", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "According to the speaker, what is required of you when you begin practising a new habit?", "options": ["A) The ability to make the process automatic.", "B) A practical timetable for implementation.", "C) A lot of conscious effort to remember to do it.", "D) Concentration on the benefits it will provide."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） A lot of conscious effort to remember to do it.\n【定位】Another science-based reason to focus on one thing at a time is that when you begin\npractising a new habit, it requires a lot of conscious effort to remember to do it.\n另一个一次只专注一件事的科学依据是，当你开始练习一个新习惯时，你需要付出大量有意\n识的努力去记住去做这件事。\n【信号】时间状语 + 需求动词。题干中的 when you begin practising a new habit 在 when you\nbegin practising a new habit 中原样复现，紧随其后的 requires a lot of conscious\neffort to remember to do it 就是答案所在。\n【替换】requires a lot of conscious effort to remember to do it ↔\nA lot of conscious effort to remember to do it\n名词短语截取\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nThe ability to make the process automatic 来自 the process is more or less mindless\nand automatic，但那是 after a while / eventually 之后的阶段，不是 begin 阶段；题干\n问的是刚开始，因此相反。\n借⽤原⽂：the process is more or less mindless and automatic\nB) 张冠李戴\nA practical timetable for implementation 把 when, where, and how you intend to\nimplement a particular behavior 里的具体计划要素（when/where/how）包装成\ntimetable，但这是关于 implementation intentions 的研究，不是开始养成新 habit 时的\n普遍要求。\n借⽤原⽂：when, where, and how you intend to implement a particular behavior\nD) 未提及\nConcentration on the benefits it will provide 中 benefits 一词在录音中未被提及，属于\n用常识编造的干扰项。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What was one of the more bizarre findings about Pluto's atmosphere?", "options": ["A) It resembles that of Earth in many inconceivable ways.", "B) It has a haze much thicker than previously believed.", "C) It is 333.4°F cooler than earlier estimates.", "D) It is far beyond any quick measurements."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It has a haze much thicker than previously believed.\n【定位】One of the more bizarre things they found was that the haze in Pluto's atmosphere was much\nthicker than previously thought.\n他们发现的一件更离奇的事情是，冥王星大气中的霾层比之前认为的要厚得多。\n【信号】题干关键词复现 + 比较级信息。录音中 One of the more bizarre things they found 与题\n干中的 finding 直接呼应，随后 was much thicker than previously thought 给出具体特\n征，正是答案所在。\n【替换】the haze in Pluto's atmosphere was much thicker than previously thought ↔\nIt has a haze much thicker than previously believed.\n同义词 + 主谓压缩\n【排除】A) 未提及\n录音通篇未说冥王星大气在‘不可思议的方面’与地球相似；A 项利用考生对地球类比的生活\n预设制造干扰，但原文没有依据。\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 项盗用了 333.4°F 与 earlier estimates 两个原词，但把原文‘最高温度为 −333.4°F（比此\n前估算更冷）’偷换成‘低了 333.4°F’，既丢掉负号又混淆了绝对温度与温度差，是典型的数\n字原词陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：333.4°F\nD) 未提及\nD 项说‘远超任何快速测量’，但原文 made some quick measurements of Pluto's\natmosphere 只是提到做了快速测量，并未说它无法测量或超出测量范围。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "What is said to determine the overall temperature on Earth?", "options": ["A) The accumulation of solid particles in the air.", "B) The distribution of gases in its atmosphere.", "C) The clouds of particles reflecting sun rays.", "D) The aggravated effects of climate change."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） The distribution of gases in its atmosphere.\n【定位】But on Earth, the overall temperature of the planet is dominated by the distribution of gases in\nour atmosphere.\n但在地球上，行星的整体温度是由大气中气体的分布所主导的。\n【信号】转折后给出地球情况的结论。录音先用 Pluto 的 haze 机制做铺垫，随后用 But on Earth\n一转，指出 the overall temperature of the planet is dominated by the distribution\nof gases in our atmosphere，直接回答题干中的 determine the overall temperature\non Earth。\n【替换】the overall temperature of the planet is dominated by the distribution of gases in our atmosphere ↔\nThe distribution of gases in its atmosphere.\n主被动转换 + 同义替换\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 项的 solid particles in the air 直接来自 solid particles get suspended in the air，但\n原文说的是地球‘雾/霾天’的成因，并非决定地球整体温度的因素，属于盗用原词答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：solid particles get suspended in the air\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 项的 clouds of particles reflecting sun rays 来自 emitting clouds of particles that\nreflect the Sun's rays into the atmosphere，但那是火山喷发后的局部现象，不是决定地\n球整体温度的主因。\n借⽤原⽂：clouds of particles that reflect the Sun's rays into the atmosphere\nD) 与原文相反\nD 项说 climate change 的加剧效应决定温度，而原文 alleviate the effects of climate\nchange 讲的是‘缓解’气候变化，并非‘加剧’；选项把 alleviating 偷换成 aggravated，极性\n相反。\n借⽤原⽂：alleviate the effects of climate change", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What does the speaker say the researchers have to wait and see?", "options": ["A) If spectrums of light can be better detected by telescope.", "B) If Pluto's hazy atmosphere can be more easily observed.", "C) If Pluto's hazy atmosphere scatters strong sunlight.", "D) If the light from the telescope confirms their idea."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） If the light from the telescope confirms their idea.\n【定位】The researchers just have to wait and see if the light from the telescope validates their\nbright idea.\n研究者们只能等待并观察，来自望远镜的光线是否会证实他们那个不错的想法。\n【信号】题干关键词 wait and see 原词复现。录音末句 The researchers just have to wait and\nsee 与题干中的 wait and see 完全对应，随后的 if 从句就是答案所在。\n【替换】if the light from the telescope validates their bright idea ↔\nIf the light from the telescope confirms their idea.\n同义词替换 + 形容词省略\n【排除】A) 答非所问\nA 项提到 spectrums of light 与 telescope，看似来自 That observatory can detect\ndifferent spectrums of light，但题干问的是‘研究者要等看什么’，而不是‘望远镜能检测什\n么’；选项把工具能力偷换成等待验证的条件。\n借⽤原⽂：detect different spectrums of light\nB) 未提及\nB 项说‘如果冥王星的霾层能被更容易观测’，但录音并未讨论观测难度是否 easier；原文只\n提到未来有机会用 James Webb 验证，没有 easier 这一比较。\nC) 答非所问\nC 项的 scatters strong sunlight 来自 as it scattered sunlight，但这是已建立的假设前提\n（haze 散射阳光），不是研究者现在要等的结果；选项把已知机制当成待验证条件。\n借⽤原⽂：as it scattered sunlight", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What do we learn from researchers' conclusion at the beginning of the talk?", "options": ["A) Parents can play a decisive role in cultivating their children's musical talent.", "B) Children are embarrassed by parents' failure to appreciate their music.", "C) Parents can mould their 8- to 10-year-old children's music taste.", "D) Children aged 8 to 10 are old enough to produce poetic lyrics."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Parents can mould their 8- to 10-year-old children's music taste.\n【定位】Parents have a small window where they can influence their 8- to 10-year-old children's\nmusic taste, researchers have concluded.\n研究人员得出结论：父母有一个短暂的窗口期，可以影响8到10岁孩子的音乐品味。\n【信号】研究结论标志词。答案句以researchers have concluded结尾，是典型的研究结论标志；同\n时句首直接给出结论内容，符合篇章开头'先给结论'的常见结构。\n【替换】influence their 8- to 10-year-old children's music taste ↔\nmould their 8- to 10-year-old children's music taste\ninfluence与mould同义替换\nParents have a small window where they can ↔ Parents can\nsmall window限定为可能性\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项中的musical talent在原文中从未出现，原文讲的是music taste；同时decisive role把\n原文a small window夸大成'决定性作用'，与原文程度不符。\n借⽤原⽂：Parents have a small window where they can influence their 8- to 10-year-old children's\nmusic taste\nB) 原词陷阱\n原文have not yet started to be embarrassed by their mother and father's\npreferences说的是'还不会因父母的偏好感到尴尬'，而选项说成'Children are\nembarrassed by parents' failure to appreciate their music'，既把对象从'父母的偏好'偷\n换成'父母不欣赏孩子的音乐'，又把否定'not yet'变成了肯定。\n借⽤原⽂：have not yet started to be embarrassed by their mother and father's preferences\nD) 原词陷阱\n原文old enough to appreciate poetic lyrics说的是'足够大去欣赏富有诗意的歌词'，选项\n却改成'produce poetic lyrics'，把appreciate偷换成produce，动作主体和方向都错了。\n借⽤原⽂：old enough to appreciate poetic lyrics", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What does Dr. Hauke Egermann say about adolescents?", "options": ["A) They start to show their own musical preference.", "B) They tend to be more influenced by their peers.", "C) They tend to gravitate away from their homes.", "D) They are more open to new genres of music."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） They tend to be more influenced by their peers.\n【定位】During adolescence, children tend to gravitate away from their parents' choices and are more\ninfluenced by their peers.\n在青春期，孩子们往往会偏离父母的选择，更受同龄人的影响。\n【信号】题干关键词复现+比较级。原文During adolescence与题干adolescents直接对应，句中\nmore influenced by their peers用比较级点明影响来源的变化，是答案信号。\n【替换】During adolescence ↔ adolescents\n名词化同义\nare more influenced by their peers ↔ tend to be more influenced by their peers\nbe动词添加趋向性\n【排除】A) 未提及\n原文说的是孩子'偏离父母的选择'，并未说他们开始展示自己的musical preference；选项\n把'父母的选择'偷换成'自己的偏好'，属于无中生有。\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项盗用了原文gravitate away from这一短语，但把对象从their parents' choices偷换成\ntheir homes，语义完全改变。\n借⽤原⽂：gravitate away from their parents' choices\nD) 张冠李戴\n原文Children have an openness to new music说的是儿童（8-10岁）对新音乐的开放态\n度，选项把它安到adolescents头上，属于群体错位。\n借⽤原⽂：Children have an openness to new music", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "What is the key to fostering good taste in later life, according to Dr. Hauke Egermann?", "options": ["A) Enabling children to be receptive to music experts' suggestions.", "B) Helping children develop the ability to manage their emotions.", "C) Familiarising children with different schools of music.", "D) Introducing children to a variety of musical styles."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Introducing children to a variety of musical styles.\n【定位】Dr. Egermann claims introducing children to a variety of musical styles is the key to fostering\ngood taste in later life.\n埃格曼博士称，让孩子接触多种多样的音乐风格是培养其日后良好品味的关键。\n【信号】专家观点+the key to结构。原文Dr. Egermann claims是专家观点标志，紧接着出现is the\nkey to fostering good taste in later life，与题干中的the key to fostering good taste in\nlater life完全对应。\n【替换】introducing children to a variety of musical styles ↔\nIntroducing children to a variety of musical styles\n核心名词短语保留\nclaims ↔ according to Dr. Hauke Egermann\nclaims与according to同义\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\n原文中从未出现music experts' suggestions这一概念；虽然receptive to their musical\nsuggestions出现过，但其对象是parents' suggestions，不是music experts'。\n借⽤原⽂：their children were far more receptive to their musical suggestions before the age of ten\nB) 原词陷阱\n原文the ability to manage their emotions more effectively确实是音乐品味广泛的好处\n之一，但它不是'the key to fostering good taste'，而是good taste带来的效果。\n借⽤原⽂：the ability to manage their emotions more effectively\nC) 偷换概念\n原文说的是a variety of musical styles（多种音乐风格），选项说成different schools of\nmusic（不同音乐流派/学派），styles与schools of music含义不同；且原文用的是\nintroducing而非familiarising。\n借⽤原⽂：a variety of musical styles", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "How can parents help children create positive memories about artists and bands?", "options": ["A) By enhancing children's appreciation of their music.", "B) By pushing their music hard enough upon children.", "C) By attending their concerts and live music events with children.", "D) By introducing their music to children as early as possible."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） By attending their concerts and live music events with children.\n【定位】They can also help create positive memories about artists and bands by going to concerts and\nlive music events together.\n他们还可以通过带孩子一起去听音乐会和现场音乐演出来帮助创造关于艺人和乐队的积极\n记忆。\n【信号】题干关键词复现+by doing方式状语。原文help create positive memories about artists\nand bands与题干完全复现，其后的by going to concerts and live music events\ntogether就是具体方式。\n【替换】by going to concerts and live music events together ↔\nBy attending their concerts and live music events with children\ngoing to与attending同义，together与with children同义\n【排除】A) 未提及\n选项中的enhancing children's appreciation of their music虽然与全文话题相关，但原文\n在P4中并未把它作为'create positive memories'的具体方法提出。\nB) 与原文相反\n原文advises parents not to push their favorite music too hard on their children明确建\n议父母不要把喜欢的音乐强行推给孩子，而选项却说'By pushing their music hard\nenough upon children'，正好与专家建议相反。\n借⽤原⽂：advises parents not to push their favorite music too hard on their children\nD) 偷换概念\n原文虽然前面提到'critical period before age ten'，但在P4关于create positive\nmemories的具体做法中，并未提及'as early as possible'这一时间策略。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) behavioural", "B) eliminate", "C) fragile", "D) global", "E) implications", "F) maximise", "G) overlapping", "H) personally", "I) presume", "J) prone", "K) prototypes", "L) reassure", "M) skeptically", "N) traits", "O) underlying"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) global\n形容词\n【词性槽】冠词与名词之间锁形容词\n【依据】2008年全球金融危机\n\"2008年全球金融危机\"是常识性固定表述；文本证据同样充分——上句 Most financial\ncrises have plenty in common 谈\"多数金融危机\"的共性，本句说这场危机 no different\n（也不例外），是在把单场危机放进普遍、整体的尺度里讨论，global 与 Most financial\ncrises 的宏阔范围呼应。\n【竞争词】A) behavioural 主题词提前抢跑\n全文确实讲行为因素，很多考生会因此把 behavioural 按在这里——但 26 空修饰的是\"危机\"的\n规模/范围维度，行为分析后文才展开；behavioural crisis 非惯用表达，behavioural 真正的家\n在 28 空 the ___ biases。\nC) fragile fragile不修饰危机\nfragile 意为\"脆弱的\"，惯用 fragile economy / system；crisis 本身是坏事，再说\"脆弱的危\n机\"逻辑别扭。\nG) overlapping 重叠的危机无文本支撑\noverlapping 意为\"重叠的\"，文中没有任何\"多场危机重叠\"的信息；2008 是一场特定危机，\noverlapping 属无中生有。\nJ) prone prone必带to不作前置定语\nprone 的惯用结构是 be prone to sth，几乎不直接放名词前作定语（a prone crisis 不成\n立）；它的正确位置在 32 空 be ___ to risk 的框架里。\nO) underlying underlying修饰原因不修饰危机\nunderlying 意为\"根本的、潜在的\"，惯用 underlying cause / reason；修饰 crisis 语义勉强，\n而它正是 27 空 another ___ cause 的标准答案。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·冠词与名词之间锁形容词"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) behavioural", "B) eliminate", "C) fragile", "D) global", "E) implications", "F) maximise", "G) overlapping", "H) personally", "I) presume", "J) prone", "K) prototypes", "L) reassure", "M) skeptically", "N) traits", "O) underlying"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) underlying\n形容词\n【词性槽】another与名词之间锁形容词\n【依据】表层共性之下的根本原因\n首段先说这场危机与多数危机有共性（银行业、过度借贷、资产泡沫），随即 But 转折：\nBut my research suggests this crisis had another __(27)__ cause——在众目所见的共性\n原因**之外/之下**还有一个\"根本的\"原因。underlying（根本的、潜藏的）正好承载 But\n的对比：表面原因人尽皆知，underlying cause 才是作者研究的新发现；破折号后\"银行从\n业者为私利操纵系统\"正是这个深层原因的内容。\n【竞争词】A) behavioural 主题词眼熟乱选\n本卷最阴的干扰：全文确实讲行为因素，behavioural cause 看似点题——但破折号引出的解释\n强调的是\"此前未被点破的深层原因\"，重在\"潜藏\"而非\"行为的\"；且 28 空 the ___ biases 只能\n由 behavioural 填（behavioural biases 是行为金融学术语），在此提前用掉它，28 空将无词\n可填。\nC) fragile 脆弱的原因搭配不通\nfragile 修饰 cause 语义不通——\"脆弱的原因\"既非惯用搭配，也与破折号后的操纵行为无关。\nG) overlapping 重叠的原因无中生有\n文中只讲\"另一个\"原因，没有任何\"多个原因重叠\"的信息，overlapping 属无中生有。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 A) —— 主题词眼熟加篇章呼应的假正当。全文主题就是行为因素，\nbehavioural cause 读起来像点题，词性也对、词义也沾边，是本卷错误率最高的选法。但\nanother __(27)__ cause—that some people in the banking sector were playing or\n\"gaming\" the system 的破折号解释强调的是\"此前未被点破的深层原因\"，且 28 空 the\n___ biases 只能由 behavioural 填（术语搭配）。防坑一句话：主题词先查它的命定搭配位\n置，别在第一眼像的空提前用掉。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·another与名词之间锁形容词"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) behavioural", "B) eliminate", "C) fragile", "D) global", "E) implications", "F) maximise", "G) overlapping", "H) personally", "I) presume", "J) prone", "K) prototypes", "L) reassure", "M) skeptically", "N) traits", "O) underlying"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) behavioural\n形容词\n【词性槽】the与名词之间锁形容词\n【依据】行为偏差渗透各级决策\nbehavioural biases（行为偏差）是行为经济学/金融学的标准术语，指人的系统性心理偏\n差；下句 Banking often attracts people with certain personality traits, including\nthose who are overconfident and take excessive risks 的\"过度自信、冒大险\"正是\nbehavioural biases 的具体表现，与空后的 permeate decision-making（渗透各级决策）\n浑然一体。\n【竞争词】C) fragile 脆弱的偏差语义错位\nfragile 修饰的是系统、经济等会\"破碎\"的实体，biases（偏差）无所谓脆弱与否；\"脆弱的偏差\n渗透决策\"语义不通。\nG) overlapping 重叠的偏差似是而非\noverlapping biases 字面上能组合，是最后的诱惑；但下文所举\"过度自信、爱冒险\"是类型不同\n的偏差而非\"互相重叠\"，且 overlapping 与\"渗透各级决策\"的普遍性描写无关——文中没有任\n何\"重叠\"信息。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·the与名词之间锁形容词"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) behavioural", "B) eliminate", "C) fragile", "D) global", "E) implications", "F) maximise", "G) overlapping", "H) personally", "I) presume", "J) prone", "K) prototypes", "L) reassure", "M) skeptically", "N) traits", "O) underlying"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) traits\n名词\n【词性槽】personality后缺中心名词\n【依据】personality traits固定搭配\npersonality traits（人格特质）是心理学与日常英语的固定搭配；更直接的证据在空后：\nincluding those who are overconfident and take excessive risks 用 including 举例\n——过度自信、冒大险正是\"人格特质\"的具体内容，例子在给空位下定义。\n【竞争词】E) implications 与personality不构成搭配\nimplications 意为\"影响、含义\"，惯用 implications of/for sth；personality implications 不\n是惯用表达，且后文所举\"过度自信、爱冒险\"是人自身的特质，不是某种\"影响\"。\nK) prototypes 原型与人格描写脱节\nprototypes 指产品或模型的\"原型、雏形\"，是具体物；银行业吸引的是具有某些人格特质的\n人，\"人格原型\"与 overconfident、take excessive risks 的描写对不上。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·personality后缺中心名词"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) behavioural", "B) eliminate", "C) fragile", "D) global", "E) implications", "F) maximise", "G) overlapping", "H) personally", "I) presume", "J) prone", "K) prototypes", "L) reassure", "M) skeptically", "N) traits", "O) underlying"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) implications\n名词\n【词性槽】the与of夹出名词\n【依据】复杂性对稳定的后果\nimplications of A for B（A对B的影响/后果）是高频双介词搭配，与空后的 of that\ncomplexity for the stability 框架完全吻合；\"他们并不完全理解那种复杂性对整个金融体\n系稳定性的影响\"，又与下句 Often they do not care 衔接自然——不理解影响，所以不\n在乎。\n【竞争词】K) prototypes 原型与抽象语境不符\nprototypes 指具体产品/模型的\"原型\"，而本空要接的是抽象概念 complexity 与 stability 之间\n的关系；\"复杂性的原型\"语义不通。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·the与of夹出名词"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) behavioural", "B) eliminate", "C) fragile", "D) global", "E) implications", "F) maximise", "G) overlapping", "H) personally", "I) presume", "J) prone", "K) prototypes", "L) reassure", "M) skeptically", "N) traits", "O) underlying"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) maximise\n动词\n【词性槽】目的不定式to后接动词原形\n【依据】操纵系统为了奖金最大化\n全文主旨即银行家为私利操纵系统：playing or \"gaming\" the system for their own\nfinancial gain。操纵系统的目的自然是让自己的奖金**最大化**，maximise their\nbonuses 与 for their own financial gain 同义呼应，主旨与搭配双重锁定。\n【竞争词】B) eliminate 逻辑指向相反\neliminate 意为\"消除\"，eliminate their bonuses＝取消自己的奖金，与 for their own\nfinancial gain 的逐利动机完全相反——典型\"正话反说\"型干扰。\nI) presume 接不了bonuses这个宾语\npresume 作\"假定\"讲时多接 that 从句或 presume sth to be；presume their bonuses（\"假定\n他们的奖金\"）语义不通，操纵系统不是为了\"假定奖金\"。\nL) reassure 宾语须为人\nreassure 的宾语通常是人（reassure sb），意为\"使某人安心\"；reassure their bonuses 搭配\n不成立，\"让奖金安心\"拟人化得毫无道理。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·目的不定式to后接动词原形"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) behavioural", "B) eliminate", "C) fragile", "D) global", "E) implications", "F) maximise", "G) overlapping", "H) personally", "I) presume", "J) prone", "K) prototypes", "L) reassure", "M) skeptically", "N) traits", "O) underlying"], "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J) prone\n形容词\n【词性槽】be与to夹出表语形容词\n【依据】be prone to固定搭配\nbe prone to sth（易于遭受……）是词典级固定搭配，\"银行业总有些部分永远容易遭受风\n险\"；且与后半句 but many bankers have come to feel immune to its potential impact\n的 immune to（对……免疫）形成对照：客观上 prone to risk、主观上 feel immune——\nbut 转折严丝合缝。\n【竞争词】A) behavioural behavioural不作表语接to\nbehavioural（行为的）只作前置定语修饰名词（behavioural biases），be behavioural to\nrisk 结构不成立。\nC) fragile fragile to搭配不地道\nfragile 语义上与 risk 沾边、最具迷惑性；但英语惯用 be fragile 单独作表语或 be vulnerable\nto，be fragile to risk 不是地道搭配——六级常考\"词义对但搭配错\"这一坑。\nG) overlapping 与risk语义不接\noverlapping（重叠的）与 risk 连用语义不通，银行部门\"重叠于风险\"不知所云。\nO) underlying 不作表语形容词接to\nunderlying（潜在的）多作前置定语（underlying cause），be underlying to risk 结构不\n成立。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 C) —— 词义沾边但搭配不地道。\"对风险脆弱\"按中文思维完全通\n顺，fragile 词性也对；但英语惯用 be prone / vulnerable to risk，be fragile to 不是地\n道搭配。防坑一句话：当中文直译很顺而英文搭配陌生时，信搭配（be prone to），不信\n直译。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·be与to夹出表语形容词"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) behavioural", "B) eliminate", "C) fragile", "D) global", "E) implications", "F) maximise", "G) overlapping", "H) personally", "I) presume", "J) prone", "K) prototypes", "L) reassure", "M) skeptically", "N) traits", "O) underlying"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) presume\n动词\n【词性槽】主语后缺谓语且接that从句\n【依据】他们认定政府必兜底\nthat 从句的内容 however recklessly banks behave, governments will always be there\nto bail them out（无论银行多鲁莽，政府总会救助）正是银行家一种想当然的认定；正因\n为 presume 如此，上句 many bankers have come to feel immune to its potential\nimpact（觉得风险伤不到自己）才成立，因果链完整。\n【竞争词】L) reassure 句式结构不成立\nreassure 的正确结构是 reassure sb that...（使某人相信……），必须带人作宾语；they\nreassure that... 缺宾语、结构不成立——两个词义虽都沾\"相信\"的边，句式直接判生死。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·主语后缺谓语且接that从句"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) behavioural", "B) eliminate", "C) fragile", "D) global", "E) implications", "F) maximise", "G) overlapping", "H) personally", "I) presume", "J) prone", "K) prototypes", "L) reassure", "M) skeptically", "N) traits", "O) underlying"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) eliminate\n动词\n【词性槽】impossible to后接动词原形\n【依据】操纵行为几乎无法消除\n主语 Gaming（操纵行为）是空位的逻辑宾语，\"银行业的操纵行为几乎不可能被__\"；下句\nThe only effective measure to end it 的 end it（终结它）正是 eliminate 的同义改写，\n语义直接锁定——只有\"消除/终结\"才接得上\"唯一有效办法\"。\n【竞争词】I) presume 与行为现象搭配无义\n\"几乎不可能去假定操纵行为\"语义不通；presume 的宾语应是某个判断或事实，不是一种行为现\n象，且 presume 更合 33 空 they ___ that 从句的句式。\nL) reassure 宾语须为人且逻辑相反\nreassure 意为\"使安心\"，宾语须是人；\"不可能去安抚操纵行为\"搭配不当，且与下句\"终结它\"的\n指向相反。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·impossible to后接动词原形"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) behavioural", "B) eliminate", "C) fragile", "D) global", "E) implications", "F) maximise", "G) overlapping", "H) personally", "I) presume", "J) prone", "K) prototypes", "L) reassure", "M) skeptically", "N) traits", "O) underlying"], "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H) personally\n副词\n【词性槽】修饰形容词liable须用副词\n【依据】personally liable法律追责搭配\n本段谈\"终结操纵的唯一有效办法是让银行家__对损失负责\"，下句 but no government has\never passed such a law 表明这是在讲法律层面的追责；personally liable（个人承担法律\n责任）是法律英语惯用搭配，\"追到个人\"才解释了为何没有政府敢立此法。\n【竞争词】M) skeptically 词义与搭配双不符\nskeptically 意为\"怀疑地\"，惯常修饰 say、look at 这类言行动词；skeptically liable 既非常见\n搭配，\"怀疑地承担责任\"语义也不成立，且与下文追责语境完全脱节。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·修饰形容词liable须用副词"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "Land Life Company's efforts fit into the global goal to restore millions of hectares of the world's deforested and degraded land.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】Bonn Challenge / millions of hectares\n【定位】With around 600 hectares replanted around the world since 2015 and another 1,100 planned\nfor this year, the company's drive fits into the Bonn Challenge, a global effort to restore 150\nmillion hectares of the world's deforested and degraded land by 2020.\n自2015年以來，該公司已在全球重植約600公頃土地，今年還計劃再植1100公頃；這一\n努力契合了「波恩挑戰」——一項到2020年恢復全球1.5億公頃被砍伐與退化土地的全球\n目標。\n【改写】Land Life Company's efforts ↔ the company's drive\n同義替換 efforts = drive\nfit into the global goal ↔ fits into the Bonn Challenge, a global effort\nglobal goal 被具體化為 Bonn Challenge\nto restore millions of hectares of the world's deforested and degraded land ↔\nto restore 150 million hectares of the world's deforested and degraded land\n數字具體化\n【辨邻】 B peppered them with their signature device 等內容聚焦 cocoon 技術與2018年造\n林，並未出現 Bonn Challenge 或百萬公頃目標。\nThe region's dry climate and depleted soils would be a recipe for disaster in your average program\nto replant trees", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "Planting trees or shrubs together with crops can help improve the yield of the land, while offering small-scale farmers a chance to make a living.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】trees or shrubs with crops / small-scale farmers\n【定位】Although not technically reforestation, agroforestry (a system of land use in which\nharvestable trees or shrubs are grown among or around crops or on pasture land, as a means\nof preserving or enhancing the productivity of the land) provides an opportunity for small-\nholder farmers to sustain their livelihoods while adding some forest cover to their farms.\n雖然嚴格來說不算重新造林，但農林業（一種土地利用體系，即在作物周圍或之間、或牧場\n上種植可採收的樹木或灌木，以保持或提高土地生產力）為小農提供了在維持生計的同時為\n農場增加些許森林覆蓋的機會。\n【改写】Planting trees or shrubs together with crops ↔\ntrees or shrubs are grown among or around crops\n動作與對象的同義改寫\nhelp improve the yield of the land ↔ enhancing the productivity of the land\nyield 與 productivity 同義\noffering small-scale farmers a chance to make a living ↔\nprovides an opportunity for small-holder farmers to sustain their livelihoods\n句式重構＋同義替換\n【辨邻】 K A global project called Breedcafs is studying how trees behave in coffee farms\n雖涉及 trees 與 crops，但聚焦尋找咖啡品種，未提及 small-holder farmers 或維持\n生計。\nA global project called Breedcafs is studying how trees behave in coffee farms", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "One reforestation project bases its argument on economic benefits and investment returns.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】economic benefits / investment returns\n【定位】Unlike Land Life Company, this region-wide project makes the economic and business case\nfor reforestation, even though they are after biodiversity conservation. 'You need to bring in\nprivate sector money,' says WRI's Vergara, who leads the initiative, 'and that capital needs to\nsee a return on their investment.' A study he led predicts Latin America would see an\nestimated net present value of about $23bn over a 50-year period if it reaches its goal.\n與 Land Life Company 不同，這個覆蓋全區域的項目為重新造林提出了經濟與商業理由，\n儘管他們也追求生物多樣性保護。「你需要引入私營部門資金，」主持該倡議的 WRI 的\nVergara 說，「而且這筆資本需要看到投資回報。」他主導的一項研究預測，若目標達成，\n拉丁美洲在50年內將獲得約230億美元的淨現值。\n【改写】One reforestation project ↔ this region-wide project\n概括指代\nbases its argument on economic benefits ↔\nmakes the economic and business case for reforestation\nargument 與 case 同義\nand investment returns ↔ that capital needs to see a return on their investment\n投資回報的同義表達\n【辨邻】 H Money can come from timber sales 等雖談經濟回報，但它是「如何賺錢」的清\n單，而非「某項目以經濟回報為論證」的陳述。\nMoney can come from timber sales in sustainably managed forests", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "With time, nature may return to its original state without human intervention if the location is appropriate.", "options": null, "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】without human intervention / appropriate location\n【定位】But in the right location, perhaps all you need is time. In the Central Pacific of Costa Rica,\nthe 330-hectare National Wildlife Refuge Baru looks nothing like the cattle ranch it was\nbefore 1987, when Jack Ewing decided to turn this large estate into an ecotourism destination.\nInstead of intervening, a friend told him to let nature have its way. Baru's former grazing\nlands are now thriving woods and the property boasts over 150 hectares of secondary forests\nwith no intervention from humans.\n但在合適的地點，或許你所需要的只是時間。在哥斯大黎加中太平洋地區，330公頃的 Baru\n國家野生動物保護區看起來已完全不像1987年之前的那片牧場——當時 Jack Ewing 決定將\n這片大產地改為生態旅遊目的地。一位朋友告訴他不要干預，讓自然自行其是。Baru 曾經\n的放牧地如今已是茂盛的林地，該地產擁有超過150公頃未經人類干預的次生林。\n【改写】With time, nature may return to its original state ↔\nBaru's former grazing lands are now thriving woods and the property boasts over 150 hectares\nof secondary forests\n語義概括\nwithout human intervention ↔ no intervention from humans\n同義改寫\nif the location is appropriate ↔ in the right location\n同義替換\n【辨邻】 C it can take decades or hundreds of years, so we are speeding things up 與題幹\n的 without human intervention 方向相反。\nit can take decades or hundreds of years, so we are speeding things up", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "According to a forest and climate expert, the revival of forest cover can take place under the necessary conditions.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】forest and climate expert / necessary conditions\n【定位】Spurred by global biodiversity loss and climate change, these groups are pushing the\nboundary of how to revive forest cover. 'It's not a theoretical proposition,' says Walter\nVergara, a forest and climate specialist at the World Resources Institute(WRI). 'It requires\nthe right incentives, the right stakeholders, the right analysis and sufficient capital, but it\ncan happen.'\n受全球生物多樣性喪失和氣候變化的驅使，這些組織正在突破如何恢復森林覆蓋的邊界。\n「這不是一個理論命題，」世界資源研究所（WRI）的森林與氣候專家 Walter Vergara\n說，「它需要正確的激勵、正確的利益相關者、正確的分析和充足的資本，但這是可以實\n現的。」\n【改写】According to a forest and climate expert ↔\nsays Walter Vergara, a forest and climate specialist at the World Resources Institute(WRI)\n身份同義替換\nthe revival of forest cover ↔ revive forest cover\n名詞化\ncan take place under the necessary conditions ↔\nIt requires the right incentives, the right stakeholders, the right analysis and sufficient capital,\nbut it can happen\n語義概括\n【辨邻】 G 'You need to bring in private sector money,' says WRI's Vergara 與題幹的 revival\nof forest cover can take place under necessary conditions 話題不同。\n'You need to bring in private sector money,' says WRI's Vergara, who leads the initiative", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "One company runs its major operations exclusively in countries where risks are not high.", "options": null, "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】risks are not high / major operations\n【定位】Vergara's partners look for safe investments, and even Land Life Company only runs major\nprojects in what they consider 'low-risk' countries. 'We tend to avoid large-scale operations in\ncountries in some parts of the Middle East or Africa where the permanence is not secured,'\nsays Ruys.\nVergara 的合作伙伴尋找安全投資，就連 Land Life Company 也只在他們認為「低風險」\n的國家開展重大項目。「我們傾向於避免在中東或非洲部分國家開展大規模行動，因為那裡\n的持久性無法得到保障，」Ruys 說。\n【改写】One company ↔ Land Life Company\n上下文指代\nruns its major operations ↔ only runs major projects\n同義替換\nexclusively in countries where risks are not high ↔ in what they consider 'low-risk' countries\n同義替換＋語序調整\n【辨邻】 G Unlike Land Life Company, this region-wide project makes the economic and\nbusiness case for reforestation 雖提及 Land Life Company，但對比的是經濟模\n式，不是風險標準。\nUnlike Land Life Company, this region-wide project makes the economic and business case\nfor reforestation", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "Left alone, nature may take scores of years or even centuries to repair itself.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】scores of years or even centuries\n【定位】'Does nature return by itself?' asks Arnout Asjes, Land Life Company's chief technology\nofficer, who oversees a mix of drone and satellite imagery, big data analysis, soil\nenhancement, QR tagging and site-specific tree configuration designs. 'Probably, but it can\ntake decades or hundreds of years, so we are speeding things up.'\n「自然能自行恢復嗎？」Land Life Company 的首席技術官 Arnout Asjes 問道，他負責監\n管無人機與衛星圖像、大數據分析、土壤改良、二維碼標籤和因地制宜的樹木配置設計。\n「可能可以，但這需要數十年或數百年，所以我們正在加速這一過程。」\n【改写】Left alone, nature may take scores of years or even centuries to repair itself ↔\n'Does nature return by itself?' asks Arnout Asjes...'Probably, but it can take decades or\nhundreds of years'\n多組同義替換疊加\n【辨邻】 M But in the right location, perhaps all you need is time 與 no intervention from\nhumans 強調的是不介入即可恢復，而非「需要數十到數百年」。\nBut in the right location, perhaps all you need is time", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "Economic returns can be derived from selling timber and non-wood products as well as carbon credits.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】timber / non-wood products / carbon credits\n【定位】Money can come from timber sales in sustainably managed forests or from harvesting 'non-\nwood products' like nuts, oils and fruits from the trees. You can take note of how much\ncarbon dioxide your forest is capturing and sell carbon credits to companies keen to offset\ntheir emissions. Or you can even grow the forest hoping that biodiversity will attract\necotourists who'd pay for lodging, birdwatching tours and meals.\n資金可以來自可持續管理森林中的木材銷售，或從樹上收穫「非木材產品」如堅果、油和水\n果。你可以記錄森林吸收了多少二氧化碳，然後把碳信用賣給希望抵消排放的公司。或者你\n甚至可以培育森林，寄望生物多樣性會吸引願意支付住宿、觀鳥旅行和餐飲費用的生態旅\n遊者。\n【改写】Economic returns ↔ Money can come from\n語義概括\nselling timber ↔ timber sales\n動名詞轉換\nnon-wood products ↔ 'non-wood products'\n原樣復現\ncarbon credits ↔ sell carbon credits\n原樣復現\n【辨邻】 I Take the German fund 12Tree for instance 舉例說明投資回報，但沒有並列出\ntimber、non-wood products、carbon credits 這三項。\nTake the German fund 12Tree for instance, one of the 20x20 partners", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "A study is under way across the world to look for crops that can grow among trees.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】Breedcafs / coffee farms\n【定位】A global project called Breedcafs is studying how trees behave in coffee farms, hoping to find\ncrop varieties that manage to grow under the shade of trees. Coffee grows naturally in such\nforests, so copying that in farms is taking the crop back to its roots. 'By reintroducing trees in\nthe landscape, we impact positively humidity, rain capture, soil conservation and biodiversity\npreservation,' says coffee expert Benoit Bertrand, who leads the project from the French\nAgricultural Research Centre for International Development. Bertrand is analysing which of\ndozens of coffee varieties fits better for this system.\n一個名為 Breedcafs 的全球項目正在研究咖啡農場中樹木的生長情況，希望找到能在樹蔭下\n生長的作物品種。咖啡天然生長於這樣的森林中，因此在農場中模仿這種做法等於讓作物回\n歸本源。「通過在景觀中重新引入樹木，我們積極影響濕度、雨水截留、土壤保護和生物多\n樣性保護，」在法國農業國際合作研究中心領導該項目的咖啡專家 Benoit Bertrand 說。\nBertrand 正在分析數十種咖啡品種中哪一種更適合這一系統。\n【改写】A study is under way across the world ↔ A global project called Breedcafs is studying\n同義改寫\nto look for crops that can grow among trees ↔\nhoping to find crop varieties that manage to grow under the shade of trees\n同義替換＋語境細化\n【辨邻】 J agroforestry (a system of land use in which harvestable trees or shrubs are\ngrown among or around crops or on pasture land) 雖涉及 crops 與 trees，但沒\n有 Breedcafs、coffee farms 或研究品種的內容。\nagroforestry (a system of land use in which harvestable trees or shrubs are grown among or around\ncrops or on pasture land)", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "Land Life Company's operations are usually located where reforestation is impossible without human intervention.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】Land Life Company / without human intervention\n【定位】The region's dry climate and depleted soils would be a recipe for disaster in your average\nprogram to replant trees, but for the Amsterdam-based Land Life Company it's an ideal\nplace. 'We typically operate where nature does not come back by itself,' says Jurrian Ruys,\nits CEO. 'We go where there are rougher conditions in terms of weather, with rough or very\nhot summers.'\n該地區乾燥的氣候和貧瘠的土壤對普通的植樹項目來說會是災難配方，但對總部位於阿姆斯\n特丹的 Land Life Company 而言卻是理想之地。「我們通常在大自然無法自行恢復的地方\n作業，」其首席執行官 Jurrian Ruys 說，「我們去氣候條件更惡劣的地方，那裡夏季炎熱\n或酷暑。」\n【改写】Land Life Company's operations ↔ We typically operate\n同義替換\nare usually located where reforestation is impossible without human intervention ↔\nwhere nature does not come back by itself\n雙重否定改寫\n【辨邻】 C 'Does nature return by itself?' asks Arnout Asjes...'Probably, but it can take\ndecades or hundreds of years, so we are speeding things up' 雖也談 nature\nreturn by itself，但沒有說 Land Life 通常在什麼地方作業。\n'Does nature return by itself?' asks Arnout Asjes...'Probably, but it can take decades or hundreds of\nyears, so we are speeding things up'\nPARAGRAPH MAP · 段落主旨图\n个段落各讲了什么\n逐段一句主旨 + 该段最具区分度的「信息指纹」，是逐句回扫定位的工作台。\nA\n介绍伊比利亚半岛森林覆蓋的历史變遷與 Matamorisca 等地景觀退化的背景。\n信息指纹 五百年前的伊比利亚森林\nB 說明 Land Life Company 在 Matamorisca 乾旱貧瘠地區的具體作業方式與 cocoon\n技術。\n信息指纹 cocoon 容器與 2018 年造林數據\nC 提出自然自行恢復可能需要極長時間，而 Land Life Company 正在加速這一過程。\n信息指纹 decades or hundreds of years\nD\n將 Land Life Company 置於全球恢復退化與砍伐區域的運動中，並引用 WRI 專家\nVergara 的觀點。\n信息指纹 WRI / Walter Vergara\nE 指出不同生態系統對造林計畫的需求各異，並說明 Land Life Company 在西班牙乾旱\n地區的沙漠化擔憂。\n信息指纹 生態系統差異與 desertification\nF 說明 Land Life Company 的全球造林規模，並將其與 Bonn Challenge 的宏大目標\n對接。\n信息指纹 Bonn Challenge 與百萬公頃目標\nG 介紹拉丁美洲 20x20 Initiative 及其以經濟回報為論證的運作邏輯。\n信息指纹 20x20 Initiative 與 $23bn\nH\n列舉森林復育的多種經濟收入來源：木材、非木材產品、碳信用與生態旅遊。\n信息指纹 non-wood products / carbon credits\nI 說明 20x20 的資金來自 impact investors，並以德國 12Tree 投資巴拿馬 Cuango\n為例。\n信息指纹 impact investors / 12Tree / Cuango\nJ 介紹 agroforestry 如何讓小農在維持生計的同時增加森林覆蓋。\n信息指纹 agroforestry / small-holder farmers\nK 介紹 Breedcafs 全球項目研究咖啡農場中樹蔭下可生長的作物品種。\n信息指纹 Breedcafs / coffee farms / Benoit Bertrand\nL 指出並非所有土地都適合造林，Land Life Company 只在「低風險」國家開展重大\n項目。\n信息指纹 low-risk countries\nM 以哥斯大黎加 Baru 保護區為例，說明在合適地點僅靠時間與不介入即可讓自然恢復。\n信息指纹 Costa Rica / Baru / 1987", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What plays an important role in determining how much self-discipline people experience?", "options": ["A) Their belief about the nature of motivation.", "B) Their use of internal energy for persisting.", "C) Their conception of self-discipline.", "D) Their ability to resist temptation."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What plays an important role in determining...询问的是某一结果的决定因素，属于'某事\n由什么造成'的事实定位型提问。\n【拆句】What plays an important role in determining how much self-discipline people\nexperience?是主谓宾结构：what作主语，plays作谓语，an important role作宾语，in\ndetermining...作后置定语修饰role。整体语法平铺直叙，无否定或比较陷阱。\n【定位】Your answer matters, as it determines how much self-discipline you experience.\n你的答案很重要，因为它决定了你体验到多少自律。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\n选项把原文关于self-discipline本性的信念，偷换成了about the nature of\nmotivation，属于概念替换。\nB) 偷换概念\n'internal energy'只是人们可能持有的一种信念内容，不是决定自律体验的因素本身。\nC) ✓ 正确\nYour answer matters ↔ Their conception of self-discipline\ndetermines how much self-discipline you experience ↔\nplays an important role in determining how much self-discipline people experience\nTheir conception of self-discipline对应'你对self-discipline本性的回答/信念'，与定位\n句完全贴合。\nD) 答非所问\n抵抗诱惑是self-discipline的一种表现形式，而不是决定人们体验到多少自律的因素。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What did years of research suggest about self-discipline?", "options": ["A) It is a depletable resource.", "B) It is bolstered through use.", "C) Its strength is dependent on willpower.", "D) Its exercise is an exhausting experience."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What did years of research suggest about self-discipline?直接询问过去研究的观点，属\n于事实细节题。\n【拆句】What did years of research suggest about self-discipline?是特殊疑问句，主语为years\nof research，谓语为suggest，about self-discipline为介词短语。无复杂从句或否定\n结构。\n【定位】For years, research in psychology pointed to self-control as a resource that is depleted with\nuse and needs to be restored through breaks, rest, or sleep.\n多年来，心理学研究把self-control视为一种会随使用而耗尽、需要通过休息、睡眠来恢复\n的资源。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\na resource that is depleted with use ↔ It is a depletable resource\nIt is a depletable resource准确对应a resource that is depleted with use。\nB) 与原文相反\n这是较新研究/积极信念的观点，不是'years of research'早期研究的结论。\nC) 无中生有\n原文并未说self-control的强度dependent on willpower，二者在这里几乎是同义\n循环。\nD) 过度推断\n原文说需要休息恢复，不等于说使用体验是'exhausting experience'（令人筋疲力尽的\n经历）。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "Why does the author ask us to reflect on our experience of meeting the goal despite initial difficulty?", "options": ["A) To show the urgency of abandoning outdated beliefs about self-control.", "B) To show the feasibility of viewing self-discipline as a force fueling itself.", "C) To support the argument for persistence in workplace accomplishments.", "D) To add evidence to consistent findings in previous psychological research."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】Why does the author ask us to reflect on...虽含why，但仍是就文中具体建议的意图提\n问，答案可在该建议句附近找到，归为细节题而非推断题。\n【拆句】Why does the author ask us to reflect on our experience of meeting the goal\ndespite initial difficulty?中，why问目的，主句为the author asks us to reflect on...，\nof meeting the goal despite initial difficulty为experience的后置定语。无否定或比较\n结构。\n【定位】Reflect on times that you had initial difficulty resisting temptation, getting started on\nsomething, or sticking with it, but pushed ahead and accomplished the task or met the goal.\nThat's the kind of experience we're talking about with this particular belief about self-\ndiscipline, and you've already had such an experience, so you've shown that it's possible.\n回想那些你起初难以抵制诱惑、难以开始或难以坚持，但最终推进并完成任务或达成目标的\n时刻。这正是我们所说的关于自律的那种特定信念所对应的体验，而且你已经有过这样的体\n验，所以你已证明这是可能的。\n【选项】A) 过度推断\n原文并未说要'abandon outdated beliefs'，只是建议补充一种积极信念，没有\nurgency 或彻底抛弃旧信念的意思。\nB) ✓ 正确\nThat's the kind of experience we're talking about with this particular belief about self-discipline\n↔ viewing self-discipline as a force fueling itself\nyou've shown that it's possible ↔ the feasibility of\nviewing self-discipline as a force fueling itself对应this particular belief，the\nfeasibility of对应you've shown that it's possible。\nC) 答非所问\n虽然文中提到workplace，但本段建议的目的不是支持'职场成就中的坚持'，而是证明自\n律信念的可行性。\nD) 张冠李戴\n定位句所支持的是'自律可自我强化'的新信念，而非previous psychological research\n中'self-control会耗尽'的一致发现。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What are we advised to do if we cannot afford to take breaks to recharge?", "options": ["A) Figure out what has gone wrong.", "B) Focus on the hardest part of our task.", "C) Exert ourselves to go on with the work.", "D) Gather new energy from people around."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What are we advised to do if we cannot afford to take breaks to recharge?询问在特\n定条件下作者的建议，属于事实细节题。\n【拆句】What are we advised to do if we cannot afford to take breaks to recharge?中，what\n作do的宾语，if引导条件状语从句，to recharge为目的状语。整体结构清晰。\n【定位】If not, remind yourself that not everyone experiences self-discipline as a depletable resource\nand that your experience might be different if you focus on gathering more energy at this\npoint rather than focusing on how difficult it is to continue or the need for a break.\n如果不能休息，提醒自己并非所有人都把自律体验为会耗尽资源；如果你此刻专注于积聚更\n多能量，而不是专注于继续有多难或需要休息，你的体验可能会不同。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n原文并未建议去'figure out what has gone wrong'，而是直接给出调整心态的建议。\nB) 与原文相反\n原文明确说不要focus on how difficult it is to continue，而该选项说focus on the\nhardest part，与作者建议相反。\nC) ✓ 正确\nfocus on gathering more energy at this point rather than focusing on how difficult it is to\ncontinue\n↔ Exert ourselves to go on with the work\nExert ourselves to go on with the work对应定位句中'focus on gathering more\nenergy...continue'的核心动作。\nD) 偷换概念\n原文说'focus on gathering more energy'，并未说从'people around'那里获取能量；\n第六段提到role model，但也不是本题条件建议的内容。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "Why does the author compare our self-discipline to a muscle?", "options": ["A) The more it is applied, the more resistance it encounters.", "B) The more it is exerted, the more proud we become.", "C) The more it is enforced, the stronger our willpower proves.", "D) The more it is exercised, the more strengthened it gets."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】Why does the author compare our self-discipline to a muscle?询问比喻的论证目的，\n答案在比喻句及其后续解释中可直接找到。\n【拆句】Why does the author compare our self-discipline to a muscle?中，why问原因/目的，\ncompare...to...是固定搭配，表示把A比作B。无复杂从句。\n【定位】Your self-discipline is akin to a muscle, and each time you resist temptation or persist in a\ntask despite a lack of motivation, you strengthen that muscle, leading to less effort and\nresistance in similar instances in the future.\n你的自律 akin to 肌肉，每次你抵制诱惑或在缺乏动力时坚持任务，你都在强化那块肌肉，\n从而在未来类似情况下减少努力和阻力。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\n原文说强化肌肉后'leading to less effort and resistance'，即阻力更少，而不是\nencounters more resistance。\nB) 张冠李戴\nproud是第六段mental reinforce的内容，不是肌肉比喻要说明的要点。\nC) 偷换概念\n原文说strengthen that muscle（强化自律本身），而选项说stronger our willpower\nproves，把主语从self-discipline换成了willpower，且语义上二者在此文中近于同义\n反复。\nD) ✓ 正确\neach time you resist temptation or persist in a task despite a lack of motivation, you strengthen\nthat muscle\n↔ The more it is exercised, the more strengthened it gets\nThe more it is exercised, the more strengthened it gets完整对应each time...you\nstrengthen that muscle的解释。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What do around half of parents of teens do according to a new study?", "options": ["A) They set their kids' phones to filter out obscene information.", "B) They try to alleviate the spread of explicit scenes on the internet.", "C) They feel concerned about their kids' viewing indecent content online.", "D) They strive to prevent their kids from spending excessive screen time."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干What do around half of parents of teens do according to a new study?询问研究\n中的具体事实，属于对单一信息的直接考查。\n【拆句】主干为What do...parents do，according to a new study为介词短语作状语，说明信息来\n源。整体是简单疑问句，无否定、比较等陷阱。\n【定位】A new study shows that about half of parents of teens are worried about their teens being\nexposed to explicit content online.\n一项新研究显示，约半数青少年父母担心自己的孩子在网上接触露骨内容。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念：措施≠已实施行为\nA项They set their kids' phones to filter out obscene information.把第二段中\nchanging the settings这类可能的缓解措施，说成是父母已经采取的行动，而原文并未\n说半数父母已这样做。\nB) 偷换概念：可能性≠实际行为\nB项They try to alleviate the spread of explicit scenes on the internet.把原文there\nare ways to alleviate the spread（存在缓解途径）替换为父母try to alleviate（正在\n尝试缓解），改变了行为主体与事实状态。\nC) ✓ 正确\nworried ↔ concerned\nbeing exposed to explicit content online ↔ viewing indecent content online\nC项They feel concerned about their kids' viewing indecent content online.准确对\n应定位句中父母worried about孩子being exposed to explicit content online。\nD) 答非所问：跨段混用话题\nD项They strive to prevent their kids from spending excessive screen time.将第三\n段以后才出现的excessive screen time话题嫁接到第二段关于explicit content的研究结\n论上，答非所问。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What does a large amount of research show about teens' excessive smartphone use?", "options": ["A) It allows them less time for meaningful activities.", "B) It renders them more lonely and low-spirited.", "C) It gives them less time for social interactions.", "D) It makes them more alert to online bullying."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干What does a large amount of research show about teens' excessive smartphone\nuse?要求找出研究所示的具体结果。\n【拆句】主干为What...research show，about teens' excessive smartphone use为介词短语限定\n范围。无复杂从句或否定。\n【定位】Ample research shows too much smartphone use produces significant anxiety, depression,\nand feelings of isolation in teens. Teens log on to social media or spend time online trying to\nmake connections, but end up feeling more alone and sad than ever.\n大量研究表明，过度使用智能手机会给青少年带来严重的焦虑、抑郁和孤独感。青少年登录\n社交媒体或上网试图建立联系，但最终感到比以往更加孤独和悲伤。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念：上位/下位词替换失当\nA项It allows them less time for meaningful activities.把第四段healthy activities\nassociated with childhood, like spending time outside, exercising, talking to\nfriends and family中的healthy activities替换为meaningful activities，缩小并改变了\n概念范围。\nB) ✓ 正确\nfeeling more alone ↔ lonely\nsad ↔ low-spirited\nfeelings of isolation ↔ lonely\nB项It renders them more lonely and low-spirited.准确概括了定位句中more alone\nand sad than ever及feelings of isolation的含义。\nC) 与原文相反：行为方向矛盾\nC项It gives them less time for social interactions.说减少了社交时间，但原文Teens\nlog on to social media or spend time online trying to make connections说明青少\n年反而花更多时间在网上进行社交尝试。\nD) 偷换概念：结果→主体态度\nD项It makes them more alert to online bullying.把原文increases instances of\nonline bullying（增加网络欺凌案例）改为more alert to online bullying（对网络欺凌\n更警觉），改变了语义关系。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What do we learn about the author's feelings regarding smartphones and screen time?", "options": ["A) They are deep-seated.", "B) They are instinctive.", "C) They are unique.", "D) They are mixed."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （态度题）\n【判型】题干What do we learn about the author's feelings regarding smartphones and\nscreen time?直接询问作者对某事物的情感态度。\n【拆句】主干为What do we learn about the author's feelings，regarding smartphones and\nscreen time为介词短语说明对象。无复杂结构。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全：只取单一态度信号\nA项They are deep-seated.（根深蒂固的）只呼应了作者对智能手机问题的强烈反感，\n但忽略了作者承认便利和理解上瘾的部分。\nB) 偷换概念：情感强烈≠本能\nB项They are instinctive.（本能的）将作者对智能手机的厌恶理解为本能反应，但原文\n明确给出了理性原因（屏幕时间、通讯便利等），并非单纯本能。\nC) 无中生有\nC项They are unique.（独特的）在原文中没有任何对应信息，作者反而说I understand\nthe addiction，暗示这种感受具有普遍性。\nD) ✓ 正确\nI wish I could throw my kids' smartphones into the nearest lake. The convenience of\ncommunication...often feels like it isn't worth the battle...I understand the addiction...I too,\nstruggle with overuse.\n↔ mixed\nD项They are mixed.用一词概括了第六段中作者既反感又理解、既看到弊端又承认便利\n的复杂情感。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "态度题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What does actress Kate Winslet think about the world of social media?", "options": ["A) It poses increasing challenges to parents trying to ensure internet safety.", "B) It scares parents because they don't really know what is on the screen.", "C) It is a sphere difficult for parents and their teens to navigate.", "D) It is full of uncertainties frightful to parents and their teens."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干What does actress Kate Winslet think about the world of social media?要求提取\n特定人物对特定事物的观点。\n【拆句】主干为What...Kate Winslet think，about the world of social media为介词短语说明观点\n对象。结构简单。\n【定位】\"I do definitely feel that the world of social media is frightening to parents because we don't\nreally know what's there,\" she said.\n她（Kate Winslet）说：我确实觉得社交媒体世界对父母来说很可怕，因为我们真的不知道\n那里面有什么。\n【选项】A) 无中生有：添加原文未出现概念\nA项It poses increasing challenges to parents trying to ensure internet safety.\n将原文frightening扩大为increasing challenges，并加入原文未提的ensure\ninternet safety。\nB) ✓ 正确\nthe world of social media is frightening to parents ↔ It scares parents\nwe don't really know what's there ↔ because they don't really know what is on the screen\nB项It scares parents because they don't really know what is on the screen.与定位\n句frightening to parents because we don't really know what's there完全对应。\nC) 张冠李戴：背景信息≠人物观点\nC项It is a sphere difficult for parents and their teens to navigate.源自第七段首句a\nnew film about a mother and daughter navigating social media use，这是电影内\n容，不是Kate对社交媒体的看法。\nD) 偷换概念：扩大态度对象范围\nD项It is full of uncertainties frightful to parents and their teens.把原文frightening\nto parents扩大为frightful to parents and their teens，增加了青少年作为恐惧主体。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-2-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What message does the author try to convey in the last paragraph?", "options": ["A) Parents have to be concerned about dangers to their teens in both real and virtual worlds.", "B) Governments have responsibilities in regulating social media and teens' smartphone use.", "C) Parents should provide guidance to their teens in the use of social media.", "D) Online dangers are as serious as problems with teenagers' mental health."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （主旨题）\n【判型】题干What message does the author try to convey in the last paragraph?要求概括最后\n一段作者想要传达的核心信息。\n【拆句】主干为What message does the author try to convey，in the last paragraph为地点状\n语。无复杂结构。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nparents don't just have to worry about real-world dangers, but all the ones sneaking online ↔\nParents have to be concerned about dangers to their teens in both real and virtual worlds.\nA项Parents have to be concerned about dangers to their teens in both real and\nvirtual worlds.与末段parents don't just have to worry about real-world dangers,\nbut all the ones sneaking online完全对应。\nB) 与原文相反\nB项Governments have responsibilities in regulating social media and teens'\nsmartphone use.认为政府有责任监管，但原文I don't think the U.S. government\nshould regulate social media and teen smartphone use明确反对政府监管。\nC) 偷换概念：态度重视≠具体指导\nC项Parents should provide guidance to their teens in the use of social media.把原\n文parents to understand and take seriously（父母应理解并认真对待）改为provide\nguidance（提供指导），改变了具体行动。\nD) 偷换概念：影响关系→比较关系\nD项Online dangers are as serious as problems with teenagers' mental health.把原\n文the effects they have on teenagers and their mental health（网络危险对青少年\n心理健康的影响）改为online dangers与mental health problems之间的严重程度比\n较，改变了逻辑关系。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "主旨题"]}]});
